/* Periodic Coast, Gym 7 - Electron Configuration and Chemical Periodicity (CHM 11510 lessons 13-14,
   Silberberg 10th ed. 8.1-8.4; Evening Exam 2). Lesson 13 (8.1-8.2): electron spin and mₛ, the Pauli
   exclusion principle, quantum-number sets for a named electron, shielding, penetration and Zeff
   (Zeff ≈ Z − core electrons), sublevel splitting and why 4s fills before 3d, aufbau order, full and
   condensed configurations (main-group, transition, inner-transition), Hund’s rule and orbital diagrams
   in words, unpaired electrons, valence vs inner electrons, identifying elements and ground / excited /
   impossible configurations, and the Cr, Cu, Mo, Ag exceptions. Lesson 14 (8.3-8.4): ion configurations
   (main-group ions, transition-metal ions losing ns first, p-block ions losing np first), isoelectronic
   species, para- vs diamagnetism, atomic and ionic radius, first and successive ionization energies
   (including the group 2/13 and 15/16 exceptions and IE tables), electron affinity, metallic character
   and the acid-base behaviour of oxides. Written in the style of Silberberg sample and end-of-chapter
   problems. Every configuration, electron count, unpaired-electron count and l-count in a key or
   distractor was checked with an aufbau script (with the Cr/Cu/Mo/Ag exceptions and ns-first ion rule);
   IE and EA values quoted are standard table values (kJ/mol) and every ranking key was checked against them. */
chemAddQuestions([
["m7-a-001",7,13,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which statement is the Pauli exclusion principle?",
 ["Electrons fill the lowest-energy orbitals available first.","Electrons occupy equal-energy orbitals singly, with parallel spins, before any pairing occurs.","No two electrons in the same atom can have the same set of four quantum numbers.","It is impossible to know both the exact position and the exact momentum of an electron.","The energy of an orbital depends only on its principal quantum number n."],2,
 "Pauli: no two electrons in one atom share all four quantum numbers (n, l, mₗ, mₛ). Two electrons in the same orbital already share n, l and mₗ, so they must differ in mₛ - which is why an orbital holds at most 2 electrons, with opposite spins. The other statements are the aufbau principle, Hund’s rule, the Heisenberg uncertainty principle, and a fact that is true only for one-electron species like hydrogen.",
 ["Each of the wrong choices is a different named rule from Chapters 7-8.","This principle is about the four quantum numbers.","It is the rule that limits an orbital to two electrons."]],
["m7-a-002",7,13,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "What is the maximum number of electrons a single orbital can hold, and what must be true of them?",
 ["1 electron","2 electrons with the same spin","6 electrons, as in a p orbital","Any number, as long as their mₗ values differ","2 electrons with opposite spins (mₛ = +½ and −½)"],4,
 "An orbital is fixed by n, l and mₗ. Only mₛ is left to differ, and it has just two values (+½, −½), so an orbital holds at most 2 electrons and they must have opposite spins (Pauli). 6 is the capacity of a p SUBLEVEL (three orbitals), not one orbital; two electrons in one orbital cannot have the same spin.",
 ["An orbital is defined by three quantum numbers. How many are left to tell its electrons apart?","mₛ has only two possible values.","Two electrons, and their spins must differ."]],
["m7-a-003",7,13,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which values can the spin quantum number mₛ take?",
 ["+½ or −½ only","0, +1 or −1","Any integer from −l to +l","+1 or −1","Any whole number from 0 to n − 1"],0,
 "The electron spin quantum number has only two values, +½ and −½ (spin up and spin down). Integers from −l to +l are the allowed mₗ values, and 0 to n − 1 are the allowed l values.",
 ["The fourth quantum number describes the electron’s spin, which has two directions.","It is not an integer.","Spin up and spin down: plus or minus one half."]],
["m7-a-004",7,13,1,"Concept","num",
 "What is the maximum number of electrons that can occupy the n = 3 principal energy level? (Enter a whole number of electrons.)",
 ["18 electrons"],18,
 "The n = 3 level has n² = 9 orbitals (one 3s, three 3p, five 3d). Each holds 2 electrons, so 2n² = 18: 3s (2) + 3p (6) + 3d (10). A common slip is 8 (only 3s and 3p, as in period 3 of the table) - but the level itself also includes the 3d sublevel.",
 ["List the sublevels that exist when n = 3.","3s, 3p and 3d: add their capacities (2, 6, 10).","2n² with n = 3."]],
["m7-a-005",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which set of quantum numbers (n, l, mₗ, mₛ) is NOT allowed for an electron in an atom?",
 ["3, 2, −2, +½","4, 0, 0, −½","2, 1, 0, +½","3, 1, −2, −½","5, 3, +3, +½"],3,
 "Rules: l = 0 to n − 1; mₗ = −l to +l; mₛ = ±½. In (3, 1, −2, −½), l = 1 allows mₗ = −1, 0, +1 only, so mₗ = −2 is impossible. Check the rest: (3, 2, −2) is a 3d electron; (4, 0, 0) is 4s; (2, 1, 0) is 2p; (5, 3, +3) is 5f - all allowed.",
 ["Check each rule in turn: l against n, then mₗ against l, then mₛ.","|mₗ| can never be larger than l.","Look for the set where l = 1 but mₗ is outside −1…+1."]],
["m7-a-006",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which set of quantum numbers (n, l, mₗ, mₛ) is NOT allowed?",
 ["1, 0, 0, −½","2, 2, 1, +½","4, 3, −3, −½","3, 2, 0, +½","2, 0, 0, +½"],1,
 "l must be less than n (l = 0 … n − 1). With n = 2, l can only be 0 or 1, so l = 2 (a ‘2d’ orbital) does not exist. The others are 1s, 4f, 3d and 2s electrons, all allowed.",
 ["Compare l with n in each set.","The largest allowed l is n − 1.","There is no 2d sublevel."]],
["m7-a-007",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Two electrons occupy the same 3p orbital. Which quantum number MUST be different for these two electrons?",
 ["n","l","mₛ","mₗ","None - electrons in the same orbital have identical quantum numbers"],2,
 "Being in the same orbital means the same n (3), same l (1) and same mₗ. By the Pauli exclusion principle they cannot share all four, so mₛ must differ: one +½, one −½. The last choice is exactly what Pauli forbids.",
 ["What three quantum numbers define one particular orbital?","The Pauli principle says the set of four cannot be identical.","Only the spin is left to differ."]],
["m7-a-008",7,13,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Which set of quantum numbers (n, l, mₗ, mₛ) could describe the LAST electron added, by the aufbau principle, to a ground-state gallium atom (Z = 31)?",
 ["4, 0, 0, +½","3, 2, +2, +½","4, 2, 0, +½","4, 1, −2, +½","4, 1, −1, +½"],4,
 "Ga = [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p¹, so the last electron goes into a 4p orbital: n = 4, l = 1, mₗ = −1, 0 or +1, mₛ = ±½. Only (4, 1, −1, +½) fits. (4, 0, 0) is a 4s electron (Ga’s 4s fills earlier); (3, 2, +2) is a 3d electron (filled before 4p); (4, 2, 0) would be 4d, which is empty; and (4, 1, −2) is impossible because mₗ cannot exceed l.",
 ["Write Ga’s condensed configuration first.","Ga is the first element of the 4p block: [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p¹.","A 4p electron has n = 4 and l = 1, with mₗ between −1 and +1."]],
["m7-a-009",7,13,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "Which set of quantum numbers (n, l, mₗ, mₛ) could describe a VALENCE electron of a ground-state sulfur atom?",
 ["3, 1, 0, −½","2, 1, 0, −½","3, 2, 0, +½","3, 1, 1, 0","4, 0, 0, +½"],0,
 "S = [Ne] 3s² 3p⁴; its valence electrons are the 3s and 3p electrons. (3, 1, 0, −½) is a 3p electron - allowed and occupied. (2, 1, 0) is a 2p CORE electron; (3, 2, 0) is a 3d orbital, empty in S; (3, 1, 1, 0) has mₛ = 0, which is never allowed; (4, 0, 0) is 4s, empty in ground-state S.",
 ["Write the configuration of S and decide which sublevels are valence.","Valence electrons of S are in n = 3, in s (l = 0) or p (l = 1) orbitals.","mₛ must be ±½, and S has no 3d or 4s electrons."]],
["m7-a-010",7,13,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which set of quantum numbers (n, l, mₗ, mₛ) describes the valence electron of a ground-state potassium atom?",
 ["3, 2, 0, +½","4, 1, 0, +½","3, 0, 0, +½","4, 0, 0, +½","4, 0, 1, +½"],3,
 "K = [Ar] 4s¹. The valence electron is in 4s: n = 4, l = 0, mₗ = 0, mₛ = +½ (or −½). (3, 2, 0) is 3d - in K the 4s orbital is lower in energy than 3d, so 4s fills first. (4, 1, 0) is 4p; (3, 0, 0) is the core 3s; (4, 0, 1) is impossible because l = 0 allows only mₗ = 0.",
 ["Potassium starts period 4.","K = [Ar] 4s¹: an s electron has l = 0.","n = 4, l = 0, mₗ = 0."]],
["m7-a-011",7,13,2,"Concept","num",
 "What is the maximum number of electrons in an atom that can have the quantum numbers n = 4 and l = 2? (Enter a whole number.)",
 ["10"],10,
 "n = 4, l = 2 is the 4d sublevel. l = 2 gives mₗ = −2, −1, 0, +1, +2: five orbitals × 2 electrons = 10. Answering 32 counts the whole n = 4 level; answering 2 counts a single orbital.",
 ["Name the sublevel first.","l = 2 is a d sublevel; count its mₗ values.","Five d orbitals, two electrons each."]],
["m7-a-012",7,13,4,"Concept","num",
 "What is the maximum number of electrons in an atom that can have BOTH n = 3 and mₗ = 0? (Enter a whole number.)",
 ["6"],6,
 "For n = 3, l can be 0, 1 or 2. Each of those sublevels contains exactly one orbital with mₗ = 0 (3s, one 3p and one 3d orbital). That is 3 orbitals × 2 electrons = 6. Answering 18 counts the whole n = 3 level; answering 2 counts only the 3s orbital.",
 ["List the l values allowed when n = 3.","Each sublevel (s, p, d) has one orbital with mₗ = 0.","3 orbitals, 2 electrons each."]],
["m7-a-013",7,13,2,"Concept","num",
 "In a ground-state neon atom (1s² 2s² 2p⁶), how many electrons have mₛ = +½? (Enter a whole number.)",
 ["5"],5,
 "Neon’s 10 electrons fill 5 orbitals completely (1s, 2s and three 2p). Each filled orbital holds one +½ and one −½ electron, so 5 electrons have mₛ = +½.",
 ["Count the filled orbitals in Ne.","Each full orbital contains one electron of each spin.","Ten electrons in five full orbitals."]],
["m7-a-014",7,13,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "How many orbitals make up the 4f sublevel, and how many electrons can it hold?",
 ["5 orbitals, 10 electrons","7 orbitals, 14 electrons","3 orbitals, 6 electrons","14 orbitals, 28 electrons","4 orbitals, 8 electrons"],1,
 "f means l = 3, so mₗ = −3 … +3: 7 orbitals, each holding 2 electrons, for 14. 5 orbitals/10 electrons is a d sublevel, 3/6 is a p sublevel. The ‘4’ in 4f is n, not the number of orbitals.",
 ["f corresponds to l = 3.","Number of orbitals in a sublevel = 2l + 1.","7 orbitals × 2 electrons."]],
["m7-a-015",7,13,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "In a many-electron atom, what does shielding (screening) mean?",
 ["The nucleus repels outer electrons more strongly as Z increases.","Electrons in the same orbital must have opposite spins.","Other electrons, especially inner ones, partly cancel the nuclear attraction felt by an outer electron, so it feels a smaller effective nuclear charge.","Outer electrons never spend any time near the nucleus.","The 4s orbital is always higher in energy than the 3d orbital."],2,
 "Shielding: electron-electron repulsion from the other electrons (mostly inner electrons) offsets part of the nuclear charge, so an outer electron feels an effective nuclear charge Zeff that is less than Z. The nucleus attracts, never repels, electrons; opposite spins is the Pauli principle; outer electrons DO penetrate toward the nucleus some of the time.",
 ["Think about what the inner electrons do to an outer electron’s view of the nucleus.","Electron-electron repulsion reduces the pull an outer electron feels.","Zeff is smaller than Z because of shielding."]],
["m7-a-016",7,13,2,"Calculation","mcq",
 "Using the estimate Zeff ≈ Z − (number of inner, core electrons), what effective nuclear charge does a valence electron of sodium (Z = 11) feel?",
 ["+11","+10","+9","+3","+1"],4,
 "Na = [Ne] 3s¹. Its 10 core electrons (1s² 2s² 2p⁶) shield the single 3s electron: Zeff ≈ 11 − 10 = +1. +11 ignores shielding entirely; +10 subtracts only one electron; +9 assumes only the two 1s electrons shield; +3 subtracts only the eight n = 2 electrons and forgets the 1s pair. (This simple count is only an estimate: the real Zeff is somewhat higher, about +2.5, because the 3s electron penetrates the core and the core does not shield perfectly.)",
 ["How many electrons are in sodium’s core?","Na = [Ne] 3s¹, so 10 core electrons.","11 − 10."]],
["m7-a-017",7,13,2,"Calculation","num",
 "Estimate the effective nuclear charge felt by a valence electron of chlorine (Z = 17), using Zeff ≈ Z − (number of core electrons). (Enter a number.)",
 ["+7"],7,
 "Cl = [Ne] 3s² 3p⁵. Core = 10 electrons (the [Ne] part). Zeff ≈ 17 − 10 = +7. Compare Na (+1): same core, six more protons, which is why Cl’s valence electrons are held much more tightly and Cl is much smaller.",
 ["Write Cl’s condensed configuration.","Core electrons = the 10 in [Ne].","17 − 10."]],
["m7-a-018",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Sodium and chlorine are both in period 3. Which valence electron feels the LARGER effective nuclear charge, and why?",
 ["A 3p electron in Cl, because Cl has six more protons but the same 10-electron core, and electrons in the same shell shield each other poorly","A 3s electron in Na, because it is closer to the nucleus","They are equal, because both are in n = 3","A 3s electron in Na, because s electrons penetrate more","A 3p electron in Cl, because Cl has more core electrons"],0,
 "Both atoms have the same [Ne] core (10 electrons). Cl has 17 protons vs Na’s 11, and the extra valence electrons in n = 3 shield each other only weakly, so Zeff rises across the period (≈ +7 vs ≈ +1). Cl does NOT have more core electrons - that is the trap. Penetration lowers the 3s energy relative to 3p within one atom, but it cannot outweigh six extra protons.",
 ["Compare the number of protons and the number of core electrons.","Both have a 10-electron core.","Zeff ≈ 17 − 10 vs 11 − 10."]],
["m7-a-019",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why is a 2s orbital LOWER in energy than a 2p orbital in a lithium atom, while 2s and 2p have the same energy in a hydrogen atom?",
 ["The 2s orbital is smaller, so it holds fewer electrons.","2p electrons have the opposite spin to 2s electrons.","In lithium, 2p has a higher principal quantum number than 2s.","A 2s electron penetrates closer to the nucleus, through the 1s shielding, so it feels a higher Zeff; hydrogen has only one electron, so there is no shielding and energy depends only on n.","The Pauli principle forbids electrons from entering 2p before 2s is full."],3,
 "Penetration: the 2s radial distribution has a small inner lobe close to the nucleus, inside the 1s electrons, so a 2s electron is less shielded and lower in energy than 2p. In H (one electron) nothing shields anything, so all n = 2 orbitals are degenerate. 2s and 2p have the same n; spin has nothing to do with sublevel energy; filling order comes from energy, not from Pauli.",
 ["What is different about a many-electron atom compared with hydrogen?","Which orbital spends more time near the nucleus, 2s or 2p?","Better penetration means less shielding and a higher Zeff."]],
["m7-a-020",7,13,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "In a many-electron atom, how do the energies of sublevels with the same n compare?",
 ["s = p = d = f","f < d < p < s","s < p < d < f","p < s < d < f","s < d < p < f"],2,
 "Shielding and penetration split the sublevels of a given n: s penetrates most and is lowest, then p, d, f. (Only in one-electron species like H are they all equal.)",
 ["Which orbital type penetrates closest to the nucleus?","s penetrates most, f least.","Energy rises as penetration falls."]],
["m7-a-021",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why does the 4s sublevel fill before 3d in potassium and calcium?",
 ["Because 4 is larger than 3, the 4s orbital must be farther from the nucleus and lower in energy.","The 4s orbital penetrates much closer to the nucleus than 3d, so in K and Ca the 4s orbital is lower in energy than 3d.","3d orbitals can only hold electrons once the 4p sublevel is full.","Hund’s rule requires s orbitals to fill before d orbitals.","The 3d sublevel does not exist until Z = 21."],1,
 "Although 4s has a larger n, its electron density penetrates close to the nucleus, so in K and Ca a 4s electron is less shielded and lower in energy than a 3d electron. Being farther from the nucleus would RAISE energy, not lower it; 3d fills before 4p, not after; Hund’s rule is about spins in equal-energy orbitals; 3d orbitals exist for any atom - they are just empty before Sc.",
 ["Think about shielding and penetration, not the n value alone.","Which orbital has electron density inside the inner shells?","4s penetrates more than 3d, so it is lower in energy in K and Ca."]],
["m7-a-022",7,13,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which statement is Hund’s rule?",
 ["When orbitals of equal energy are available, electrons occupy them singly with parallel spins before any pairing occurs.","No two electrons in an atom can have the same four quantum numbers.","Electrons are added to the lowest-energy orbitals first.","An orbital can hold at most two electrons.","Electrons pair up in the first orbital of a sublevel before moving to the next one."],0,
 "Hund’s rule: in a set of degenerate (equal-energy) orbitals, place one electron in each with parallel spins before pairing any. That minimizes electron-electron repulsion. The others are Pauli, aufbau, a consequence of Pauli, and the exact opposite of Hund’s rule.",
 ["This rule is about orbitals of EQUAL energy.","It decides whether electrons pair or spread out.","Spread out first, spins parallel, then pair."]],
["m7-a-023",7,13,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which statement about shielding and penetration is FALSE?",
 ["Inner electrons shield outer electrons much more effectively than electrons in the same sublevel shield each other.","For the same n, an s electron penetrates closer to the nucleus than a p electron.","The effective nuclear charge felt by valence electrons increases from left to right across a period.","Shielding by inner electrons increases across a period, which is why atoms get smaller from left to right.","In a hydrogen atom, the 3s, 3p and 3d orbitals have the same energy."],3,
 "The FALSE statement: across a period the number of inner (core) electrons stays the same - every period-3 atom has the [Ne] core - while Z rises by one each step. So Zeff increases and the atoms shrink; shielding by inner electrons does not increase. All the other statements are true (same-shell electrons shield poorly; s penetrates best; Zeff rises across a period; in one-electron H only n matters).",
 ["Look for the statement whose reasoning is backwards.","Does the number of core electrons change across period 3?","Core stays constant, Z rises, so Zeff rises - shielding is not what grows."]],
["m7-a-024",7,13,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which statement is the aufbau principle?",
 ["An orbital holds at most two electrons.","Electrons pair up only after each orbital in a sublevel has one electron.","Cations lose their highest-n electrons first.","The 3d sublevel is always filled before 4s.","Electrons are added one at a time to the lowest-energy available orbitals, building the atom up one proton and one electron at a time."],4,
 "Aufbau (German ‘building up’): build each atom from the previous one by adding a proton and an electron, putting the electron in the lowest-energy available orbital. The others are Pauli, Hund, the ion rule for transition metals, and a reversal of the real 4s-then-3d order.",
 ["The word means ‘building up’.","It is about which orbital the next electron goes into.","Lowest available energy first."]],
["m7-a-025",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "In the aufbau filling order, which sublevel fills immediately after 5p?",
 ["5d","6s","4f","6p","4d"],1,
 "The order is … 4p 5s 4d 5p 6s 4f 5d 6p … - after 5p comes 6s, which starts period 6 (Cs, Ba). 4d fills before 5p (period 5 transition metals). 4f and 5d come after 6s; 6p is last of the three.",
 ["Use the periodic table: what block comes after the 5p row ends at Xe?","Period 6 starts with Cs and Ba.","Cs and Ba are s-block elements in period 6."]],
["m7-a-026",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which list gives sublevels in the correct aufbau (filling) order?",
 ["3s 3p 3d 4s 4p","4s 4p 3d 5s 4d","5s 5p 4d 6s 4f","4p 5s 4d 5p 6s","6s 6p 5d 4f 7s"],3,
 "Correct order: 1s 2s 2p 3s 3p 4s 3d 4p 5s 4d 5p 6s 4f 5d 6p 7s … So ‘4p 5s 4d 5p 6s’ is right. The others put 3d before 4s, 4p before 3d, 5p before 4d, and 6p before 5d and 4f.",
 ["Read the order off the periodic table row by row.","A d sublevel always fills after the next s and before the next p.","(n−1)d sits between ns and np."]],
["m7-a-027",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "What is the full ground-state electron configuration of sulfur (Z = 16)?",
 ["1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁴","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3d⁴","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹ 3p⁵","1s² 2s² 2p⁸ 3s² 3p²"],0,
 "Fill 16 electrons in order: 1s² (2) 2s² (4) 2p⁶ (10) 3s² (12) 3p⁴ (16). The 3p⁶ version has 18 electrons (it is S²⁻); 3d⁴ skips 3p; 3s¹ 3p⁵ is an excited state; 2p⁸ is impossible (a p sublevel holds only 6).",
 ["Count to 16 electrons, filling in aufbau order.","After 1s 2s 2p (10 electrons) come 3s and 3p.","10 + 2 + 4."]],
["m7-a-028",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "What is the condensed ground-state electron configuration of iron (Z = 26)?",
 ["[Ar] 3d⁸","[Ar] 4s² 4p⁶","[Ar] 4s² 3d⁶","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁷","[Kr] 4s² 3d⁶"],2,
 "After [Ar] (18 electrons) come 4s² and then 3d: 18 + 2 + 6 = 26, so Fe = [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶. [Ar] 3d⁸ ignores that 4s fills first; 4p before 3d is the wrong order; 4s¹ 3d⁷ is not an exception for Fe (only Cr and Cu in period 4); [Kr] is the wrong core (36 electrons).",
 ["Start from the noble gas before iron.","Fe is the 6th element of the 3d series.","[Ar] 4s² then 6 d electrons."]],
["m7-a-029",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "What is the condensed ground-state electron configuration of bromine (Z = 35)?",
 ["[Ar] 4s² 4p⁵","[Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4p⁷","[Kr] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p⁵","[Ar] 4s² 3d⁹ 4p⁶","[Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p⁵"],4,
 "18 (Ar) + 2 (4s) + 10 (3d) + 5 (4p) = 35, so Br = [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p⁵. Forgetting the 3d¹⁰ gives only 25 electrons; 4p⁷ is impossible; [Kr] already contains these electrons; 3d⁹ 4p⁶ is an unfilled d set with a filled p - not the ground state.",
 ["Bromine is in period 4, after the transition metals.","Period-4 p-block elements carry a filled 3d¹⁰.","[Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ then 5 p electrons."]],
["m7-a-030",7,13,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "What is the condensed ground-state electron configuration of tin (Sn, Z = 50)?",
 ["[Kr] 5s² 5p²","[Kr] 5s² 4d¹⁰ 5p²","[Kr] 5s² 4d¹⁰ 5p⁴","[Ar] 5s² 4d¹⁰ 5p²","[Kr] 5s² 3d¹⁰ 5p²"],1,
 "Sn is in period 5, group 14. 36 (Kr) + 2 (5s) + 10 (4d) + 2 (5p) = 50. Forgetting 4d gives 40 electrons; 5p⁴ is tellurium (52); [Ar] is the wrong core; the d electrons in period 5 are 4d, not 3d (3d is already inside [Kr]).",
 ["Find the noble gas before Sn and its period.","In period 5 the d sublevel that fills is 4d.","[Kr] 5s² 4d¹⁰ 5p? - group 14 has two p electrons."]],
["m7-a-031",7,13,4,"Concept","mcq",
 "What is the condensed ground-state electron configuration of lead (Pb, Z = 82)?",
 ["[Xe] 6s² 5d¹⁰ 6p²","[Xe] 6s² 4f¹⁴ 6p²","[Xe] 6s² 4d¹⁰ 4f¹⁴ 6p²","[Xe] 6s² 4f¹⁴ 5d¹⁰ 6p²","[Kr] 6s² 4f¹⁴ 5d¹⁰ 6p²"],3,
 "Period 6 fills 6s, then 4f (lanthanides), then 5d, then 6p: 54 (Xe) + 2 + 14 + 10 + 2 = 82. Leaving out 4f¹⁴ (68 electrons) or 5d¹⁰ (72) is the common slip; 4d is already inside [Xe]; [Kr] is the wrong core.",
 ["Period 6 includes the f-block.","Order after [Xe]: 6s, 4f, 5d, 6p.","Add 2 + 14 + 10 + 2 to 54."]],
["m7-a-032",7,13,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "What is the condensed ground-state electron configuration of the lanthanide neodymium (Nd, Z = 60)?",
 ["[Xe] 6s² 4f⁴","[Xe] 6s² 5d⁴","[Xe] 4f⁶","[Xe] 6s² 4f¹⁴","[Xe] 6s² 5f⁴"],0,
 "After [Xe] (54) the next electrons go into 6s (2) and then 4f: 60 − 56 = 4, so Nd = [Xe] 6s² 4f⁴. The lanthanides are the f-block of period 6 and fill 4f (n − 2), not 5d or 5f. 4f¹⁴ would be 70 electrons; [Xe] 4f⁶ skips 6s.",
 ["Nd is in the f-block, period 6.","In period 6 the f sublevel that fills is 4f (n − 2).","[Xe] 6s² plus four more electrons."]],
["m7-a-033",7,13,2,"Trap","mcq",
 "What is the ground-state electron configuration of chromium (Cr, Z = 24)?",
 ["[Ar] 4s² 3d⁴","[Ar] 3d⁶","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵","[Ar] 4s² 4p⁴","[Ar] 3d⁵ 4p¹"],2,
 "Cr is an exception: [Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵. Moving one electron from 4s into 3d gives a half-filled d set and six unpaired electrons, which is lower in energy. [Ar] 4s² 3d⁴ is what the plain aufbau order predicts - the classic trap. The other options either skip 4s or put electrons in 4p before 3d is full.",
 ["Chromium is one of the two period-4 exceptions.","Half-filled d sublevels are especially stable.","One 4s electron moves to make 3d⁵."]],
["m7-a-034",7,13,2,"Trap","mcq",
 "What is the ground-state electron configuration of copper (Cu, Z = 29)?",
 ["[Ar] 4s² 3d⁹","[Ar] 3d¹¹","[Ar] 4s² 3d⁸ 4p¹","[Kr] 4s¹ 3d¹⁰","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d¹⁰"],4,
 "Cu is an exception: [Ar] 4s¹ 3d¹⁰ - one 4s electron moves into 3d to complete a filled d sublevel. [Ar] 4s² 3d⁹ is the aufbau prediction (the trap); 3d¹¹ is impossible (d holds 10); 4s² 3d⁸ 4p¹ is an excited state; [Kr] is the wrong core.",
 ["Copper is the other period-4 exception besides chromium.","A completely filled d sublevel is extra stable.","4s¹ with 3d¹⁰."]],
["m7-a-035",7,13,4,"Trap","num",
 "How many unpaired electrons does a ground-state chromium atom (Z = 24) have? (Enter a whole number.)",
 ["6"],6,
 "Cr = [Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵. The 4s electron is unpaired, and Hund’s rule puts the five 3d electrons one in each d orbital, all unpaired: 1 + 5 = 6. Writing the aufbau version [Ar] 4s² 3d⁴ gives 4 - the trap.",
 ["Use the exception configuration, not the plain aufbau one.","Cr = [Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵.","Count the lone 4s electron plus five singly-occupied d orbitals."]],
["m7-a-036",7,13,2,"Trap","mcq",
 "What is the ground-state electron configuration of molybdenum (Mo, Z = 42), which sits directly below chromium?",
 ["[Kr] 5s² 4d⁴","[Kr] 5s¹ 4d⁵","[Kr] 5s¹ 4d¹⁰","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵","[Kr] 4d⁶"],1,
 "Mo follows the same pattern as Cr: [Kr] 5s¹ 4d⁵ (half-filled 4d set). 5s² 4d⁴ is the plain aufbau prediction; 5s¹ 4d¹⁰ is silver (47 electrons); [Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵ is chromium itself; [Kr] 4d⁶ omits the 5s electron.",
 ["Mo is in the same group as Cr.","Elements in the same group often share the exception.","[Kr] 5s¹ 4d⁵."]],
["m7-a-037",7,13,2,"Trap","mcq",
 "What is the ground-state electron configuration of silver (Ag, Z = 47)?",
 ["[Kr] 5s² 4d⁹","[Kr] 5s¹ 4d⁵","[Kr] 4d¹¹","[Kr] 5s¹ 4d¹⁰","[Xe] 5s¹ 4d¹⁰"],3,
 "Ag is in copper’s group and follows the same exception: [Kr] 5s¹ 4d¹⁰ (filled 4d). [Kr] 5s² 4d⁹ is the aufbau prediction; 5s¹ 4d⁵ is Mo; 4d¹¹ is impossible; [Xe] (54 electrons) comes after Ag.",
 ["Ag is directly below Cu.","Cu = [Ar] 4s¹ 3d¹⁰.","Same pattern one period down."]],
["m7-a-038",7,13,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which statement about ground-state chromium and copper atoms is TRUE?",
 ["Cr is [Ar] 4s² 3d⁴ and has 4 unpaired electrons.","Cu is [Ar] 4s² 3d⁹ and has 1 unpaired electron.","Cr has 6 unpaired electrons and Cu has 1, because each moves one 4s electron into 3d to reach a half-filled or filled d set.","Cr and Cu are both diamagnetic because their d sublevels are half-filled or filled.","Cr is [Ar] 3d⁶, because d electrons are lower in energy than 4s."],2,
 "Cr = [Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵ (1 + 5 = 6 unpaired) and Cu = [Ar] 4s¹ 3d¹⁰ (only the 4s electron is unpaired). The first two choices use the aufbau configurations, which are wrong for these two elements (the Cu one even gives the right number of unpaired electrons from the wrong configuration). Both atoms are paramagnetic, and Cr keeps one 4s electron.",
 ["Write the real configurations first, then count unpaired electrons.","Cr = [Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵, Cu = [Ar] 4s¹ 3d¹⁰.","Any unpaired electron makes a species paramagnetic."]],
["m7-a-039",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "How many unpaired electrons does a ground-state nitrogen atom have, and why?",
 ["1, because two of the three 2p electrons pair up","5, because all five valence electrons are unpaired","0, because the 2s and 2p electrons pair with each other","3, because the Pauli principle forbids pairing in a p sublevel","3, because Hund’s rule puts one electron in each 2p orbital with parallel spins"],4,
 "N = 1s² 2s² 2p³. By Hund’s rule the three 2p electrons go one per orbital with parallel spins, giving 3 unpaired electrons (the 2s pair is paired). Pauli does not forbid pairing - it only limits an orbital to two electrons of opposite spin - so the 3 with the Pauli reason is the right number for the wrong reason.",
 ["Write N’s configuration and look at the 2p sublevel.","Three electrons, three equal-energy 2p orbitals.","Hund: spread out before pairing."]],
["m7-a-040",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "In the ground-state orbital diagram of oxygen (1s² 2s² 2p⁴), the 1s and 2s boxes each hold a pair. How are the four 2p electrons arranged?",
 ["Two 2p orbitals each hold a pair; the third 2p orbital is empty.","One 2p orbital holds a pair; the other two each hold one electron, with OPPOSITE spins.","Four 2p orbitals each hold one electron.","One 2p orbital holds a pair; the other two each hold one electron, with PARALLEL spins.","One 2p orbital holds all four electrons."],3,
 "Hund’s rule: put one electron in each of the three 2p orbitals (parallel spins), then the fourth pairs up: ↑↓ ↑ ↑. So O has 2 unpaired electrons. Two pairs and an empty orbital violates Hund’s rule; opposite spins on the singles is not the lowest-energy arrangement; there are only three 2p orbitals; an orbital cannot hold four electrons (Pauli).",
 ["Hund first, then pair.","Three electrons go in singly; where does the fourth go?","↑↓ ↑ ↑ with the two singles parallel."]],
["m7-a-041",7,13,2,"Concept","num",
 "How many unpaired electrons does a ground-state iron atom (Fe, Z = 26) have? (Enter a whole number.)",
 ["4"],4,
 "Fe = [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶. The 4s pair is paired. For 3d⁶, five electrons go one per d orbital, and the sixth pairs with one of them: ↑↓ ↑ ↑ ↑ ↑, so 4 unpaired. Answering 6 forgets that a d set has only five orbitals; answering 2 pairs the electrons up too early.",
 ["Only the partially filled sublevel matters.","Fe has 3d⁶: five d orbitals, six electrons.","One pair and four singles."]],
["m7-a-042",7,13,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which orbital diagram (described in words) violates the Pauli exclusion principle?",
 ["Carbon: 2p has two electrons in separate orbitals with parallel spins.","Nitrogen: 2p has two electrons paired in one orbital and one electron in another; the third 2p orbital is empty.","Boron: 2s holds a pair and one 2p orbital holds one electron.","Oxygen: 2p has one pair and two single electrons with opposite spins.","Helium: the 1s orbital holds two electrons with the same spin."],4,
 "Two electrons in the same orbital with the same spin would share all four quantum numbers - forbidden by Pauli. The nitrogen and oxygen diagrams violate HUND’S rule (they are excited states, not impossible). The carbon and boron diagrams are correct ground states.",
 ["Pauli is about two electrons in ONE orbital.","Which diagram has two electrons in one orbital that are not opposite in spin?","Same orbital + same spin = identical quantum numbers."]],
["m7-a-043",7,13,2,"Concept","fill",
 "Which element has the ground-state configuration [Ne] 3s² 3p⁴? (Type the element symbol.)",
 ["S","sulfur","sulphur"],null,
 "[Ne] = 10 electrons, plus 3s² 3p⁴ = 6 more, for 16 electrons: Z = 16, sulfur. Period 3 (n = 3), group 16 (six valence electrons).",
 ["Add up the electrons: 10 in the neon core plus the rest.","10 + 2 + 4 = 16.","Z = 16 is the element below oxygen."]],
["m7-a-044",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which element has the ground-state configuration [Ar] 4s² 3d⁷?",
 ["Fe","Co","Ni","Mn","Rh"],1,
 "18 + 2 + 7 = 27 electrons: cobalt. Fe is 3d⁶, Ni is 3d⁸, Mn is 3d⁵; Rh is in cobalt’s group but in period 5 (it would have a [Kr] core).",
 ["Count the electrons.","18 + 2 + 7.","Z = 27 is cobalt’s atomic number."]],
["m7-a-045",7,13,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which element has the ground-state configuration 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p³?",
 ["P","V","As","Sb","Ge"],2,
 "2 + 2 + 6 + 2 + 6 + 2 + 10 + 3 = 33 electrons: arsenic (period 4, group 15). P has the same outer p³ but in n = 3; V is [Ar] 4s² 3d³ (the ‘3’ belongs to d, not p); Sb is the 5p³ element; Ge is 4p².",
 ["Add every superscript.","The last sublevel, 4p³, tells you period 4, third p-block column.","Total 33 electrons."]],
["m7-a-046",7,13,3,"Trap","fill",
 "Which element has the ground-state configuration [Kr] 5s¹ 4d¹⁰? (Type the element symbol.)",
 ["Ag","silver"],null,
 "36 + 1 + 10 = 47 electrons: silver. This is one of the exceptions - the aufbau prediction for Ag would be [Kr] 5s² 4d⁹ - but both describe Z = 47. Do not be thrown by the single s electron: it is not an excited state, it is the real ground state.",
 ["Count the electrons regardless of how they are arranged.","36 + 1 + 10.","Z = 47 is directly below copper."]],
["m7-a-047",7,13,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "The configuration 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹ 3p¹ represents:",
 ["an excited state of magnesium","the ground state of aluminum","an excited state of aluminum","the ground state of magnesium","an impossible configuration, because 3p cannot hold an electron while 3s is not full"],0,
 "Count: 2 + 2 + 6 + 1 + 1 = 12 electrons, so this is magnesium (Z = 12). Ground-state Mg is [Ne] 3s²; here one 3s electron has been promoted to 3p, so it is an EXCITED state - allowed, just not lowest in energy. It is not aluminum (13 electrons). Promoting an electron to a higher orbital is never impossible; only breaking Pauli or a sublevel capacity is.",
 ["Count the electrons to identify the element.","12 electrons = Mg. Is the lowest-energy orbital available filled first?","An electron moved up from 3s to 3p: excited, not impossible."]],
["m7-a-048",7,13,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which configuration is IMPOSSIBLE (not merely an excited state)?",
 ["1s² 2s¹ 2p¹","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d¹","1s¹ 2s¹","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s³","[Ne] 3s² 3p³ 4s¹"],3,
 "An s sublevel has one orbital and holds at most 2 electrons, so 3s³ breaks the Pauli principle - impossible. The others are excited states: Be with 2s → 2p promotion, K with its 4s electron in 3d, He with one electron in 2s, and S with a 3p electron promoted to 4s.",
 ["Check sublevel capacities: s 2, p 6, d 10.","Excited = an electron in a higher orbital; impossible = too many electrons in a sublevel.","Look for an s sublevel with more than two electrons."]],
["m7-a-049",7,13,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Classify each configuration as ground state, excited state or impossible: (i) 1s² 2s² 2p⁵ 3s¹ (ii) [Ne] 3s² 3p⁶ 4s¹ (iii) 1s² 2p⁷",
 ["(i) ground, (ii) excited, (iii) impossible","(i) excited, (ii) excited, (iii) excited","(i) impossible, (ii) ground, (iii) excited","(i) excited, (ii) impossible, (iii) impossible","(i) excited, (ii) ground, (iii) impossible"],4,
 "(i) 10 electrons = Ne, but one 2p electron sits in 3s: excited. (ii) [Ne] 3s² 3p⁶ 4s¹ = [Ar] 4s¹ = potassium’s ground state (4s fills before 3d). (iii) 2p⁷ exceeds the p capacity of 6: impossible. A common slip is calling (ii) excited because it ‘skips’ 3d - but 4s is lower in energy than 3d in K.",
 ["Identify each element by counting electrons, then check whether the lowest orbitals are full.","Remember 4s fills before 3d.","A p sublevel can hold at most 6 electrons."]],
["m7-a-050",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "How many valence electrons does a selenium atom (Se, Z = 34) have?",
 ["34","16 (4s, 3d and 4p)","4","6","2"],3,
 "Se = [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p⁴. For a main-group element the valence electrons are the outer ns and np electrons: 2 + 4 = 6 (group 16 → last digit 6). The filled 3d¹⁰ is part of the inner electrons. 4 counts only the p electrons; 2 counts only the s electrons.",
 ["Selenium is a main-group (p-block) element in group 16.","For main-group atoms, valence = outer s + outer p.","4s² 4p⁴."]],
["m7-a-051",7,13,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "How many inner (core) electrons and how many valence electrons does a bromine atom (Z = 35) have?",
 ["18 inner, 17 valence","30 inner, 5 valence","28 inner, 7 valence","10 inner, 25 valence","33 inner, 2 valence"],2,
 "Br = [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p⁵. Valence = 4s² 4p⁵ = 7. Inner = the 18 electrons of [Ar] plus the filled 3d¹⁰ = 28. Counting 3d as valence gives 17; counting only the 4p (5) or only the 4s (2) misses the other outer electrons.",
 ["Write Br’s configuration and separate the outer (n = 4) electrons.","A filled (n − 1)d¹⁰ in a p-block element counts as inner.","18 + 10 inner; 2 + 5 valence."]],
["m7-a-052",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "How many valence electrons does a gallium atom (Ga, Z = 31) have?",
 ["1","13","31","3","2"],3,
 "Ga = [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p¹; valence = 4s² 4p¹ = 3 (group 13). The 3d¹⁰ is a filled inner sublevel. 13 counts the d electrons too (2 + 10 + 1); 1 counts only 4p; 2 counts only 4s.",
 ["Ga is in group 13.","The filled 3d¹⁰ is not valence for a p-block element.","4s² 4p¹."]],
["m7-a-053",7,13,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "An element has the ground-state configuration [Kr] 5s² 4d⁵. Which block, period and group is it in?",
 ["d-block, period 5, group 7","d-block, period 4, group 7","p-block, period 5, group 15","d-block, period 5, group 5","s-block, period 5, group 2"],0,
 "The last sublevel filled is 4d, so it is a d-block (transition) element. The period is the highest n present: 5 (from 5s). Group = s + d electrons = 2 + 5 = 7 (it is technetium). Period 4 is the trap of reading n from the 4d sublevel - a d-block element’s period number is one more than the n of its d sublevel; group 5 counts only the d electrons; an s-block element would end in 5s.",
 ["The last sublevel filled gives the block.","The period is the largest n in the configuration.","For a transition metal, group = ns + (n−1)d electrons."]],
["m7-a-054",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "An atom has the ground-state configuration [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p⁴. What are its period and group?",
 ["Period 3, group 16","Period 4, group 4","Period 5, group 16","Period 4, group 14","Period 4, group 16"],4,
 "Highest n = 4, so period 4. It is in the p-block with 4s² 4p⁴: 6 valence electrons, group 16 (it is selenium). Group 4 counts only the p electrons; period 5 or period 3 miscounts the shells; group 14 would be 4p².",
 ["Period = highest principal quantum number.","p-block group = 10 + (s + p electrons).","6 outer electrons, p-block → group 16."]],
["m7-a-055",7,13,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "In which block of the periodic table are the lanthanides and actinides?",
 ["s-block","p-block","f-block","d-block","They are not in any block."],2,
 "The lanthanides and actinides (inner transition elements) fill the 4f and 5f sublevels, n − 2 for their periods, so they make up the f-block.",
 ["Which sublevel fills across the two separated rows at the bottom of the table?","They are the ‘inner’ transition elements.","4f and 5f."]],
["m7-a-056",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why does period 4 of the periodic table contain 18 elements?",
 ["Because n = 4 can hold 2n² = 32 electrons.","Because in period 4 the 4s (2), 3d (10) and 4p (6) sublevels fill: 2 + 10 + 6 = 18.","Because the 4s, 4p, 4d and 4f sublevels all fill in period 4.","Because the 3d sublevel holds 18 electrons.","Because period 4 is where the f-block begins."],1,
 "Each period corresponds to the sublevels filled in it. Period 4 fills 4s, 3d and 4p, holding 2 + 10 + 6 = 18 electrons, so it has 18 elements (K to Kr). 4d and 4f fill in later periods; 32 is the capacity of the n = 4 level, not the length of period 4.",
 ["Which sublevels fill between Ar and Kr?","4s, then 3d, then 4p.","Add their capacities."]],
["m7-a-057",7,13,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which period-3 element has THREE unpaired electrons in its ground state?",
 ["Al","Si","S","P","Cl"],3,
 "P = [Ne] 3s² 3p³: by Hund’s rule the three 3p electrons occupy separate orbitals, so 3 unpaired. Al (3p¹) has 1, Si (3p²) 2, S (3p⁴) 2, Cl (3p⁵) 1.",
 ["Three unpaired electrons need a half-filled p set.","Which period-3 element ends in 3p³?","Group 15."]],
["m7-a-058",7,13,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "A ground-state atom has a configuration ending in 4s² 3d⁵. Which element is it, and how many unpaired electrons does it have?",
 ["Mn, 5 unpaired","Cr, 6 unpaired","Mn, 7 unpaired","Fe, 4 unpaired","Mn, 3 unpaired"],0,
 "[Ar] 4s² 3d⁵ = 18 + 7 = 25 electrons: manganese. The 4s pair is paired and the five 3d electrons occupy separate orbitals: 5 unpaired. 7 counts the paired 4s electrons; Cr is [Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵ (only one 4s electron); 3 pairs d electrons too early.",
 ["Count electrons to identify the element.","[Ar] + 7 = 25.","The 4s pair is paired; 3d⁵ has one electron per orbital."]],
["m7-a-059",7,13,4,"Trap","num",
 "How many electrons in a ground-state molybdenum atom (Mo, Z = 42) have the quantum number l = 2? (Enter a whole number.)",
 ["15"],15,
 "l = 2 means d electrons. Mo = [Kr] 5s¹ 4d⁵ (an exception, like Cr). The [Kr] core contains a filled 3d¹⁰. So d electrons = 10 (3d) + 5 (4d) = 15. Traps: using 5s² 4d⁴ gives 14; forgetting the 3d inside the core gives 5.",
 ["l = 2 is the d sublevel. Which d sublevels does Mo have?","Mo is an exception: [Kr] 5s¹ 4d⁵, and [Kr] includes 3d¹⁰.","3d¹⁰ inside the core plus 4d⁵."]],
["m7-a-060",7,13,3,"Concept","num",
 "How many electrons in a ground-state germanium atom (Ge, Z = 32) have the quantum number l = 1? (Enter a whole number.)",
 ["14"],14,
 "l = 1 means p electrons. Ge = 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p². p electrons: 2p⁶ + 3p⁶ + 4p² = 14. Counting only the valence 4p gives 2.",
 ["l = 1 is a p sublevel.","Write the full configuration and add every p superscript.","6 + 6 + 2."]],
["m7-a-061",7,14,2,"Trap","mcq",
 "What is the ground-state electron configuration of the Fe²⁺ ion?",
 ["[Ar] 4s² 3d⁴","[Ar] 4s² 3d⁶","[Ar] 3d⁶","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵","[Ar] 3d⁸"],2,
 "Fe = [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶. A transition metal loses its ns electrons BEFORE its (n − 1)d electrons, so Fe²⁺ loses both 4s electrons: [Ar] 3d⁶. [Ar] 4s² 3d⁴ removes the d electrons (the classic trap - the last electrons in are not the first out); [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶ is neutral Fe; [Ar] 3d⁸ adds electrons instead of removing them.",
 ["Start from neutral Fe.","Which electrons leave first when a transition metal ionizes?","The two 4s electrons go; 3d⁶ stays."]],
["m7-a-062",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "What is the ground-state electron configuration of the Fe³⁺ ion?",
 ["[Ar] 4s² 3d³","[Ar] 3d⁶","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁴","[Ar] 3d⁹","[Ar] 3d⁵"],4,
 "Fe = [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶. Remove the two 4s electrons first, then one 3d electron: [Ar] 3d⁵ (half-filled, 5 unpaired electrons). [Ar] 4s² 3d³ removes d electrons first; [Ar] 3d⁶ is Fe²⁺; [Ar] 3d⁹ adds electrons.",
 ["Begin with Fe = [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶.","Remove 4s electrons first, then 3d.","Three electrons gone: both 4s and one 3d."]],
["m7-a-063",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "What is the ground-state electron configuration of Cu⁺?",
 ["[Ar] 3d¹⁰","[Ar] 4s² 3d⁸","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁹","[Ar] 4s² 3d⁹","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d¹⁰"],0,
 "Cu = [Ar] 4s¹ 3d¹⁰ (an exception). Cu⁺ loses the single 4s electron: [Ar] 3d¹⁰, a filled d sublevel (diamagnetic). [Ar] 4s¹ 3d¹⁰ is neutral Cu; [Ar] 4s² 3d⁹ is the wrong (aufbau) form of neutral Cu; the others remove d electrons before the 4s electron.",
 ["Write neutral Cu correctly first - it is an exception.","Cu = [Ar] 4s¹ 3d¹⁰.","Remove the 4s electron."]],
["m7-a-064",7,14,4,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which correctly describes the Cu²⁺ ion?",
 ["[Ar] 4s² 3d⁷, paramagnetic","[Ar] 3d⁹, paramagnetic with 1 unpaired electron","[Ar] 3d⁹, diamagnetic","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁸, paramagnetic with 3 unpaired electrons","[Ar] 3d¹⁰, diamagnetic"],1,
 "Cu = [Ar] 4s¹ 3d¹⁰. Cu²⁺ loses the 4s electron and one 3d electron: [Ar] 3d⁹. Nine d electrons in five orbitals = four pairs + one single, so 1 unpaired electron: paramagnetic. Any odd-electron species must have an unpaired electron, so ‘3d⁹, diamagnetic’ cannot be right; [Ar] 3d¹⁰ is Cu⁺; the 4s-containing choices remove 3d electrons before 4s.",
 ["Write Cu correctly, then remove 4s before 3d.","Cu²⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁹.","Nine electrons in five orbitals leave one unpaired."]],
["m7-a-065",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which correctly describes the Zn²⁺ ion?",
 ["[Ar] 4s² 3d⁸, paramagnetic","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁹, paramagnetic","[Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰, diamagnetic","[Ar] 3d¹⁰, diamagnetic","[Ar] 3d⁸, paramagnetic"],3,
 "Zn = [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰. Zn²⁺ loses the two 4s electrons: [Ar] 3d¹⁰, all paired, so diamagnetic. [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ is neutral Zn; [Ar] 4s² 3d⁸ removes 3d electrons first (trap) and would wrongly be paramagnetic.",
 ["Zn = [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰.","4s electrons leave first.","A filled 3d¹⁰ has no unpaired electrons."]],
["m7-a-066",7,14,2,"Concept","num",
 "How many unpaired electrons does the Mn²⁺ ion have in its ground state? (Enter a whole number.)",
 ["5"],5,
 "Mn = [Ar] 4s² 3d⁵. Mn²⁺ loses the two 4s electrons: [Ar] 3d⁵. Five d electrons, one in each d orbital (Hund): 5 unpaired. Removing 3d electrons instead ([Ar] 4s² 3d³) would give the wrong answer 3.",
 ["Write Mn, then remove the 4s electrons.","Mn²⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁵.","Five electrons in five d orbitals."]],
["m7-a-067",7,14,3,"Trap","num",
 "How many unpaired electrons does the Co²⁺ ion have in its ground state? (Enter a whole number.)",
 ["3"],3,
 "Co = [Ar] 4s² 3d⁷. Co²⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁷ (the 4s electrons leave first). Seven d electrons: five go in singly, two pair up → 2 pairs + 3 singles = 3 unpaired. The trap [Ar] 4s² 3d⁵ would give 5.",
 ["Remove 4s electrons first.","Co²⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁷.","Five singles, then two of them pair: 5 − 2."]],
["m7-a-068",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "What is the ground-state electron configuration of the Co³⁺ ion?",
 ["[Ar] 4s² 3d⁴","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵","[Ar] 3d⁶","[Ar] 3d⁷","[Ar] 3d⁴"],2,
 "Co = [Ar] 4s² 3d⁷ (27 electrons). Co³⁺ has 24 electrons: remove both 4s electrons and one 3d electron → [Ar] 3d⁶. [Ar] 3d⁷ is Co²⁺; [Ar] 4s² 3d⁴ removes d electrons first; [Ar] 3d⁴ removes five electrons.",
 ["Co³⁺ has 27 − 3 = 24 electrons.","Remove the two 4s electrons, then one 3d.","[Ar] plus six d electrons."]],
["m7-a-069",7,14,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "What is the configuration of Cr³⁺, and how many unpaired electrons does it have?",
 ["[Ar] 4s² 3d¹, 1 unpaired","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d², 3 unpaired","[Ar] 3d⁵, 5 unpaired","[Ar] 3d³, 1 unpaired","[Ar] 3d³, 3 unpaired"],4,
 "Cr = [Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵. Cr³⁺ (21 electrons) loses the 4s electron and two 3d electrons: [Ar] 3d³, and by Hund’s rule all three are unpaired. [Ar] 4s² 3d¹ starts from the wrong neutral configuration and removes d first; [Ar] 4s¹ 3d² starts from the right one but still removes d before 4s; [Ar] 3d⁵ removes only one electron; ‘3d³, 1 unpaired’ pairs electrons against Hund’s rule.",
 ["Use the correct (exception) configuration of Cr.","Remove the 4s electron, then two 3d electrons.","Three d electrons in three separate orbitals."]],
["m7-a-070",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which correctly describes the Ti⁴⁺ ion?",
 ["[Ar]; it is isoelectronic with argon and diamagnetic","[Ar] 4s²; diamagnetic","[Ar] 3d²; paramagnetic","[Ar] 4s¹ 3d¹; paramagnetic","[Ar] 4s² 3d²; paramagnetic"],0,
 "Ti = [Ar] 4s² 3d². Removing four electrons (both 4s, then both 3d) leaves [Ar]: 18 electrons, all paired, isoelectronic with argon. [Ar] 3d² is Ti²⁺; [Ar] 4s² removes the d electrons first; [Ar] 4s² 3d² is neutral Ti.",
 ["Ti has 22 electrons; Ti⁴⁺ has 18.","Remove 4s then 3d.","Nothing beyond the argon core is left."]],
["m7-a-071",7,14,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "A student writes the configuration of Fe²⁺ as [Ar] 4s² 3d⁴. What is wrong, and how many unpaired electrons does Fe²⁺ really have?",
 ["Nothing is wrong; it has 4 unpaired electrons.","Electrons should be added, not removed: Fe²⁺ is [Ar] 4s² 3d⁸ with 2 unpaired electrons.","The 4s electrons are removed first, giving [Ar] 3d⁶ with 6 unpaired electrons.","The 4s electrons are removed first, giving [Ar] 3d⁶ with 4 unpaired electrons.","One 4s and one 3d electron are removed, giving [Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵ with 6 unpaired electrons."],3,
 "Transition-metal cations lose ns electrons before (n − 1)d: Fe = [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶ → Fe²⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁶. Six d electrons in five orbitals = one pair + four singles: 4 unpaired. Note the student’s wrong configuration also happens to give 4 unpaired - the count matching does not make the configuration right. 6 unpaired forgets there are only five d orbitals; a 2+ ion has fewer electrons, not more.",
 ["Which electrons leave first: 4s or 3d?","Fe²⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁶.","Five d orbitals hold six electrons as one pair plus four singles."]],
["m7-a-072",7,14,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Which pair of species has the same number of electrons (is isoelectronic)?",
 ["Fe²⁺ and Mn²⁺","Fe³⁺ and Mn²⁺","Cu²⁺ and Zn²⁺","Co³⁺ and Fe³⁺","Cr³⁺ and Mn³⁺"],1,
 "Count electrons (Z − charge): Fe³⁺ = 26 − 3 = 23 and Mn²⁺ = 25 − 2 = 23; both are [Ar] 3d⁵. The others: Fe²⁺ 24 vs Mn²⁺ 23; Cu²⁺ 27 vs Zn²⁺ 28; Co³⁺ 24 vs Fe³⁺ 23; Cr³⁺ 21 vs Mn³⁺ 22.",
 ["Electrons in an ion = Z − charge.","Look up Z for each element and subtract the charge.","Mn is 25, Fe is 26."]],
["m7-a-073",7,14,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "The sulfide ion, S²⁻, is isoelectronic with which noble gas?",
 ["Ne","Kr","Ar","He","S²⁻ is not isoelectronic with any noble gas."],2,
 "S has 16 electrons; S²⁻ has 18: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶, the configuration of argon. Main-group ions gain or lose electrons to reach the nearest noble gas.",
 ["Count the electrons in S²⁻.","16 electrons plus 2 more.","Which noble gas has 18 electrons?"]],
["m7-a-074",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which ion is NOT isoelectronic with neon?",
 ["N³⁻","F⁻","Na⁺","Al³⁺","K⁺"],4,
 "Neon has 10 electrons. N³⁻ (7 + 3), F⁻ (9 + 1), Na⁺ (11 − 1) and Al³⁺ (13 − 3) all have 10. K⁺ has 19 − 1 = 18 electrons - it is isoelectronic with argon.",
 ["Count electrons in each ion.","Anions gain, cations lose electrons.","Which one has 18 electrons instead of 10?"]],
["m7-a-075",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "What is the ground-state electron configuration of Al³⁺?",
 ["1s² 2s² 2p⁶","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁴","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s²","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p¹","1s² 2s² 2p³"],0,
 "Al = [Ne] 3s² 3p¹. Al³⁺ loses all three n = 3 electrons (3p first, then 3s) leaving 1s² 2s² 2p⁶, isoelectronic with Ne. 3s² is Al⁺; 3s² 3p¹ is neutral Al; 3s² 3p⁴ adds electrons; 2p³ removes core electrons.",
 ["Al³⁺ has 13 − 3 = 10 electrons.","Remove the 3p electron and then both 3s electrons.","Same as neon."]],
["m7-a-076",7,14,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "What is the ground-state electron configuration of Sn²⁺ (tin is [Kr] 5s² 4d¹⁰ 5p²)?",
 ["[Kr] 4d¹⁰ 5p²","[Kr] 5s² 4d⁸ 5p²","[Kr] 4d¹⁰","[Kr] 5s² 4d¹⁰","[Kr] 5s² 4d¹⁰ 5p⁴"],3,
 "Electrons are removed from the highest n first, and within that shell p before s. Sn’s outermost electrons are 5p², so Sn²⁺ = [Kr] 5s² 4d¹⁰. Removing the 5s pair instead (overusing ‘s comes out first’) gives the wrong [Kr] 4d¹⁰ 5p²; [Kr] 4d¹⁰ is Sn⁴⁺; removing 4d electrons is never first; 5p⁴ adds electrons.",
 ["The rule is: remove from the highest n first.","In n = 5, the 5p electrons are higher in energy than 5s.","Lose the two 5p electrons."]],
["m7-a-077",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "What is the ground-state electron configuration of Ga³⁺ (gallium is [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p¹)?",
 ["[Ar] 4s² 3d⁸","[Ar] 3d¹⁰","[Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p⁴","[Ar] 3d⁷","[Ar]"],1,
 "Remove the three n = 4 electrons (4p¹, then 4s²): Ga³⁺ = [Ar] 3d¹⁰, 28 electrons. The 3d¹⁰ stays - it is an inner sublevel. Removing 3d electrons gives [Ar] 4s² 3d⁸; removing everything past [Ar] would be Ga¹³⁺; 4p⁴ adds electrons.",
 ["Ga³⁺ has 31 − 3 = 28 electrons.","Remove the n = 4 electrons: 4p first, then 4s.","The filled 3d¹⁰ stays."]],
["m7-a-078",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which ion is diamagnetic?",
 ["Zn²⁺","Cu²⁺","Fe²⁺","Ni²⁺","Co²⁺"],0,
 "Diamagnetic = all electrons paired. Zn²⁺ = [Ar] 3d¹⁰ (all paired). Cu²⁺ 3d⁹ (1 unpaired), Fe²⁺ 3d⁶ (4), Ni²⁺ 3d⁸ (2), Co²⁺ 3d⁷ (3) are all paramagnetic.",
 ["Write each ion’s d count (4s leaves first).","A diamagnetic ion needs a completely filled (or empty) d set.","Which one is 3d¹⁰?"]],
["m7-a-079",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which species is paramagnetic?",
 ["Ca","Zn","Cu⁺","Sc³⁺","V³⁺"],4,
 "V = [Ar] 4s² 3d³ → V³⁺ = [Ar] 3d²: two unpaired electrons, paramagnetic. Ca ([Ar] 4s²), Zn ([Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰), Cu⁺ ([Ar] 3d¹⁰) and Sc³⁺ ([Ar]) have all electrons paired.",
 ["Write each configuration and look for a partly filled sublevel.","Remember 4s leaves first for transition-metal ions.","V³⁺ = [Ar] 3d²."]],
["m7-a-080",7,14,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "A species is PARAMAGNETIC if it:",
 ["has all electrons paired, so it is slightly repelled by a magnetic field","is a metal","has at least one unpaired electron, so it is attracted into a magnetic field","has an odd atomic number","has a completely filled d sublevel"],2,
 "Paramagnetic: one or more unpaired electrons, attracted by a magnetic field. Diamagnetic: all electrons paired, slightly repelled. Being a metal does not decide it (Zn is diamagnetic); odd Z alone does not decide it for ions (Cu⁺ has odd Z but is diamagnetic); a filled d set gives diamagnetism, not paramagnetism.",
 ["It is about unpaired electrons.","Unpaired spins act like tiny magnets.","At least one unpaired electron → attracted."]],
["m7-a-081",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which NEUTRAL atom is diamagnetic in its ground state?",
 ["Na","Al","O","Mg","Fe"],3,
 "Mg = [Ne] 3s²: every electron is paired, so diamagnetic. Na (3s¹) and Al (3p¹) have 1 unpaired electron, O (2p⁴) has 2, and Fe (3d⁶) has 4.",
 ["Write each outer configuration.","Which atom ends with a completely filled sublevel?","[Ne] 3s²."]],
["m7-a-082",7,14,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Rank these ions in order of INCREASING number of unpaired electrons: Zn²⁺, Cr³⁺, Ni²⁺, Mn²⁺",
 ["Zn²⁺ < Cr³⁺ < Ni²⁺ < Mn²⁺","Zn²⁺ < Ni²⁺ < Cr³⁺ < Mn²⁺","Mn²⁺ < Cr³⁺ < Ni²⁺ < Zn²⁺","Ni²⁺ < Zn²⁺ < Mn²⁺ < Cr³⁺","Zn²⁺ < Ni²⁺ < Mn²⁺ < Cr³⁺"],1,
 "Remove 4s first: Zn²⁺ 3d¹⁰ → 0; Ni²⁺ 3d⁸ → 2; Cr³⁺ 3d³ → 3; Mn²⁺ 3d⁵ → 5. So Zn²⁺ < Ni²⁺ < Cr³⁺ < Mn²⁺. Ranking Cr³⁺ below Ni²⁺ comes from thinking more d electrons means more unpaired; the descending order is the reverse.",
 ["Find the d-electron count of each ion.","Zn²⁺ 3d¹⁰, Ni²⁺ 3d⁸, Cr³⁺ 3d³, Mn²⁺ 3d⁵.","Unpaired: 0, 2, 3, 5."]],
["m7-a-083",7,14,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which species has the MOST unpaired electrons in its ground state?",
 ["Fe³⁺","Mn²⁺","Cr","Co²⁺","Cr³⁺"],2,
 "Cr = [Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵: 6 unpaired. Fe³⁺ and Mn²⁺ are both [Ar] 3d⁵ with 5 - tempting because 3d⁵ ‘is the maximum for d’, but neutral Cr adds an unpaired 4s electron. Co²⁺ (3d⁷) has 3 and Cr³⁺ (3d³) has 3.",
 ["Count the unpaired electrons in every sublevel, not just d.","Cr is an exception with a 4s¹ electron.","5 in 3d plus 1 in 4s."]],
["m7-a-084",7,14,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "An ion M²⁺ has the ground-state configuration [Ar] 3d⁶. Which element is M?",
 ["Cr","Mn","Ti","Co","Fe"],4,
 "[Ar] 3d⁶ = 24 electrons. The neutral atom has 2 more: 26 electrons = Fe (Fe = [Ar] 4s² 3d⁶; it loses its 4s pair). Cr has 24 electrons (forgot the charge); Ti would come from subtracting the charge instead of adding it; Co²⁺ is 3d⁷; Mn²⁺ is 3d⁵.",
 ["Count the ion’s electrons.","Neutral atom electrons = ion electrons + charge.","24 + 2 = 26."]],
["m7-a-085",7,14,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "A period-4 transition-metal ion X³⁺ is paramagnetic with FIVE unpaired electrons. What is X?",
 ["Mn","Fe","Cr","Co","V"],1,
 "Five unpaired electrons in a 3d ion means 3d⁵. X³⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁵ has 23 electrons, so X has 26: iron (Fe³⁺). Mn³⁺ is 3d⁴ (4 unpaired) - Mn²⁺ is the 3d⁵ manganese ion, which is the trap. Cr³⁺ is 3d³ (3), Co³⁺ 3d⁶ (4), V³⁺ 3d² (2).",
 ["What d count gives 5 unpaired electrons?","3d⁵ with a 3+ charge.","23 + 3 = 26 electrons."]],
["m7-a-086",7,14,4,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which statement about Ti²⁺ and V³⁺ is TRUE?",
 ["Ti²⁺ is [Ar] 4s² and V³⁺ is [Ar] 4s²; both are diamagnetic.","They have different numbers of electrons.","Both are [Ar] 3d², but Ti²⁺ is diamagnetic.","Both are [Ar] 3d², are isoelectronic, and are paramagnetic with 2 unpaired electrons each.","Ti²⁺ is [Ar] 3d² and V³⁺ is [Ar] 4s², so they are not isoelectronic."],3,
 "Ti (22) − 2 = 20 electrons; V (23) − 3 = 20 electrons. Removing 4s first gives [Ar] 3d² for both: isoelectronic, two unpaired electrons each (Hund), paramagnetic. Keeping the 4s² and removing 3d is the usual trap.",
 ["Count electrons in each ion.","Both have 20 electrons; remove 4s first.","Two d electrons in separate orbitals."]],
["m7-a-087",7,14,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "How many unpaired electrons do a nickel atom (Ni, [Ar] 4s² 3d⁸) and a Ni²⁺ ion have in their ground states?",
 ["Ni has 2, Ni²⁺ has 2","Ni has 2, Ni²⁺ has 4","Ni has 0, Ni²⁺ has 2","Ni has 8, Ni²⁺ has 6","Ni has 2, Ni²⁺ has 0"],0,
 "Ni: 4s² paired; 3d⁸ = three pairs + two singles → 2 unpaired. Ni²⁺ loses the 4s pair: [Ar] 3d⁸ → still 2 unpaired. Removing two 3d electrons instead ([Ar] 4s² 3d⁶) would wrongly give 4. Counting all d electrons as unpaired gives 8 and 6.",
 ["Do Ni first: which sublevel is partly filled?","Ni²⁺ loses 4s, not 3d.","Both have a 3d⁸ set."]],
["m7-a-088",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which species has the ground-state configuration 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶?",
 ["Na⁺","Ne","Cl⁻","Br⁻","Mg²⁺"],2,
 "That configuration has 18 electrons. Cl⁻ has 17 + 1 = 18. Na⁺, Ne and Mg²⁺ have 10 electrons; Br⁻ has 36.",
 ["Count the electrons in the configuration.","18 electrons.","Which ion has 18 electrons?"]],
["m7-a-089",7,14,1,"Concept","fill",
 "Which noble gas is isoelectronic with the Ca²⁺ ion? (Type the element symbol.)",
 ["Ar","argon"],null,
 "Ca has 20 electrons; Ca²⁺ has 18 = argon’s configuration, 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶.",
 ["Count electrons: Z − charge.","20 − 2 = 18.","The noble gas with Z = 18."]],
["m7-a-090",7,14,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Across a period from left to right, atomic radius generally:",
 ["increases, because more electrons are added","increases, because more shells are added","decreases, because shielding by inner electrons increases","decreases, because the effective nuclear charge increases while electrons are added to the same shell","stays the same, because n does not change"],3,
 "Across a period electrons enter the same shell while Z rises by one each step; the core stays the same, so Zeff increases and pulls the shell in. More electrons do not mean bigger here, no new shells are added, and inner shielding is constant across a period.",
 ["What changes across a period: Z, n, or the core?","Same shell, same core, more protons.","Zeff rises, radius falls."]],
["m7-a-091",7,14,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Down a group, atomic radius generally:",
 ["decreases, because Z increases","increases, because the outer electrons occupy a higher n (a larger shell)","stays the same, because the number of valence electrons is the same","increases, because Zeff increases","decreases, because shielding decreases"],1,
 "Each step down a group adds a shell: the valence electrons have a larger n and are farther from the nucleus, so atoms get bigger even though Z increases. Zeff on the outer electrons changes only slightly down a group; the new shell dominates.",
 ["What happens to n from one period to the next?","A new shell is added.","Higher n → larger orbital → larger atom."]],
["m7-a-092",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Rank Na, Mg, P and Cl in order of INCREASING atomic radius.",
 ["Cl < P < Mg < Na","Na < Mg < P < Cl","Mg < Na < Cl < P","Cl < P < Na < Mg","P < Cl < Mg < Na"],0,
 "All four are in period 3; radius decreases left to right as Zeff rises. Order of position: Na, Mg, P, Cl, so smallest to largest is Cl < P < Mg < Na. The reverse order (Na < … < Cl) is the confusion of radius with ionization energy.",
 ["All four are in the same period.","Radius decreases left to right.","Smallest is the farthest right."]],
["m7-a-093",7,14,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which atom is the LARGEST?",
 ["Li","K","Na","Be","Rb"],4,
 "Radius increases down a group and decreases across a period. Rb is lowest in group 1 among these (period 5), so it is largest. K is period 4, Na period 3, Li and Be period 2 (and Be is smaller than Li).",
 ["Largest atoms are at the bottom left.","Which element is in the highest period?","Rb is in period 5."]],
["m7-a-094",7,14,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Rank the halogens F, Cl, Br and I in order of INCREASING atomic radius.",
 ["I < Br < Cl < F","F < Br < Cl < I","F < Cl < Br < I","Cl < F < Br < I","F < Cl < I < Br"],2,
 "Down group 17 each element adds a shell, so F < Cl < Br < I. The reverse is the IE/electronegativity order.",
 ["Same group - what changes?","Each step down adds a shell.","Top is smallest."]],
["m7-a-095",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Rank Be, Mg, Ca and K in order of INCREASING atomic radius.",
 ["K < Ca < Mg < Be","Be < Mg < K < Ca","Mg < Be < Ca < K","Be < Mg < Ca < K","Be < Ca < Mg < K"],3,
 "Down group 2: Be < Mg < Ca. K is in the same period as Ca but one place to the left, so K > Ca. Order: Be < Mg < Ca < K. Putting Ca above K reverses the across-a-period trend.",
 ["Use the group trend for Be, Mg, Ca.","Compare K and Ca with the period trend.","K is to the left of Ca in period 4."]],
["m7-a-096",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which element has the SMALLEST atomic radius?",
 ["Ga","O","S","As","Se"],1,
 "Smallest atoms are at the upper right. O is in period 2 (only two shells); S, Ga, As and Se are in periods 3-4. Among the period-4 atoms Se would be smallest, and S is smaller than Se, but O is smaller still.",
 ["Look for the element in the highest row (smallest n).","Radius increases down a group.","O is the only period-2 element listed."]],
["m7-a-097",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Rank F, O, S and Se in order of DECREASING atomic radius.",
 ["Se > S > O > F","F > O > S > Se","S > Se > O > F","Se > S > F > O","O > F > S > Se"],0,
 "Down group 16: Se > S > O. Across period 2: O > F. So Se > S > O > F. ‘F > O’ is the reversed period trend; ‘S > Se’ is the reversed group trend.",
 ["Use the group trend for O, S, Se.","Then compare O and F across period 2.","F is to the right of O, so it is smaller."]],
["m7-a-098",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why is the Na⁺ ion much smaller than a Na atom?",
 ["Na⁺ has more protons than Na.","Removing an electron increases shielding of the remaining electrons.","Na⁺ has lost its only 3s electron, so its outermost electrons are in n = 2, and the 10 remaining electrons are pulled by the same 11 protons with less repulsion.","Na⁺ has gained an electron.","Cations are always larger than their parent atoms, so the premise is false."],2,
 "Na = [Ne] 3s¹ → Na⁺ = [Ne]. Losing the 3s electron removes the whole n = 3 shell, and the same nuclear charge (+11) now holds fewer electrons (less repulsion), so the ion is much smaller. The number of protons does not change; a cation LOSES electrons; cations are smaller, not larger.",
 ["What happens to sodium’s outer shell when it forms Na⁺?","Na⁺ = [Ne]: the n = 3 shell is empty.","Fewer shells and the same protons."]],
["m7-a-099",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which is larger, a Cl atom or a Cl⁻ ion, and why?",
 ["Cl, because Cl⁻ has more protons","Cl, because adding an electron increases Zeff","They are the same size because the nuclear charge is unchanged","Cl⁻, because it has one more proton","Cl⁻, because the added electron increases electron-electron repulsion while the nuclear charge stays +17"],4,
 "Cl⁻ has 18 electrons held by the same 17 protons. The extra electron increases repulsion in the valence shell and lowers the pull per electron, so the cloud expands: anions are larger than their atoms. Ions do not gain protons; Zeff per electron goes down, not up.",
 ["Does forming an anion change the number of protons?","More electrons, same nuclear charge.","Extra repulsion spreads the cloud out."]],
["m7-a-100",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺ and Mg²⁺ each have 10 electrons. Rank them in order of DECREASING radius.",
 ["Mg²⁺ > Na⁺ > F⁻ > O²⁻","O²⁻ > F⁻ > Na⁺ > Mg²⁺","F⁻ > O²⁻ > Mg²⁺ > Na⁺","Na⁺ > Mg²⁺ > O²⁻ > F⁻","They are all the same size because they all have 10 electrons."],1,
 "In an isoelectronic series the electron count is fixed, so size depends on the nuclear charge: O (8 protons) holds 10 electrons least tightly, Mg (12) most tightly. O²⁻ > F⁻ > Na⁺ > Mg²⁺. Same electron count does not mean same size.",
 ["The electron count is the same - what differs?","More protons pulling on the same 10 electrons → smaller.","Order by Z: O 8, F 9, Na 11, Mg 12."]],
["m7-a-101",7,14,4,"Concept","mcq",
 "Rank S²⁻, Cl⁻, K⁺ and Ca²⁺ in order of INCREASING radius.",
 ["S²⁻ < Cl⁻ < K⁺ < Ca²⁺","K⁺ < Ca²⁺ < S²⁻ < Cl⁻","Cl⁻ < S²⁻ < Ca²⁺ < K⁺","Ca²⁺ < K⁺ < Cl⁻ < S²⁻","Ca²⁺ < K⁺ < S²⁻ < Cl⁻"],3,
 "All four have 18 electrons (isoelectronic with Ar). The more protons, the smaller: Ca (20) < K (19) < Cl (17) < S (16). So Ca²⁺ < K⁺ < Cl⁻ < S²⁻. Cl⁻ vs S²⁻ trips people up: S has fewer protons, so S²⁻ is larger.",
 ["Check that they are isoelectronic first.","Same 18 electrons; compare Z.","Highest Z is smallest."]],
["m7-a-102",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which ion is the LARGEST?",
 ["F⁻","Al³⁺","N³⁻","O²⁻","Na⁺"],2,
 "All five have 10 electrons. The one with the fewest protons holds them least tightly: N³⁻ (7 protons) is largest. Al³⁺ (13 protons) is smallest.",
 ["Count electrons - they are all the same.","Size in an isoelectronic series is decided by Z.","Lowest Z = largest."]],
["m7-a-103",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which iron ion is smaller, Fe²⁺ or Fe³⁺, and why?",
 ["Fe²⁺, because it has more electrons","They are the same size because both are iron.","Fe²⁺, because a 3d⁶ configuration is more compact","Fe³⁺ is larger because it has a higher charge.","Fe³⁺, because the same 26 protons pull on fewer electrons (23 vs 24), with less electron-electron repulsion"],4,
 "For ions of the same element, the higher the positive charge, the fewer the electrons and the smaller the ion: Fe³⁺ (23 e⁻) is smaller than Fe²⁺ (24 e⁻). More electrons makes an ion bigger, not smaller.",
 ["Same nucleus, different numbers of electrons.","Which has fewer electrons?","Fewer electrons, same protons → smaller."]],
["m7-a-104",7,14,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which species is the SMALLEST?",
 ["Na⁺","Mg²⁺","K⁺","S²⁻","Cl⁻"],1,
 "K⁺, S²⁻ and Cl⁻ all have 18 electrons in three shells; Na⁺ and Mg²⁺ have only 10 electrons in two shells, so they are smaller. Between Na⁺ (11 p) and Mg²⁺ (12 p), Mg²⁺ holds its 10 electrons more tightly. K⁺ is tempting because K has a higher Z, but its 18 electrons fill one more shell.",
 ["First sort by number of electron shells.","Na⁺ and Mg²⁺ have only n = 1 and n = 2 occupied.","Of those two, more protons → smaller."]],
["m7-a-105",7,14,4,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which size comparison is CORRECT?",
 ["Li⁺ is larger than Li","F is larger than F⁻","Mg²⁺ is larger than Na⁺","Se²⁻ is larger than S²⁻","Al³⁺ is larger than Al"],3,
 "Se²⁻ and S²⁻ are both in group 16 with the same charge; Se²⁻ has an extra shell, so it is larger. The rest are reversed: cations are smaller than their atoms (Li⁺, Al³⁺), anions larger (F⁻), and in the isoelectronic pair Na⁺/Mg²⁺ the higher-Z ion Mg²⁺ is smaller.",
 ["Use three rules: cation smaller, anion larger, isoelectronic → more protons smaller.","For two ions of the same charge in one group, compare shells.","Se is below S."]],
["m7-a-106",7,14,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "The first ionization energy (IE₁) of an element is the energy required to:",
 ["add one mole of electrons to one mole of gaseous atoms","remove one mole of electrons from one mole of the solid element","remove the outermost electron from each atom in one mole of gaseous atoms in their ground state","remove all of the electrons from one mole of gaseous atoms","remove one mole of electrons from one mole of gaseous anions"],2,
 "IE₁: M(g) → M⁺(g) + e⁻, for one mole of gaseous atoms. It is always positive (energy must be absorbed). Adding an electron is electron affinity; it must be gaseous atoms, not a solid; only one electron per atom is removed.",
 ["Ionization makes a positive ion.","The atoms must be isolated - in the gas phase.","M(g) → M⁺(g) + e⁻."]],
["m7-a-107",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which equation represents the first ionization of magnesium?",
 ["Mg(s) → Mg⁺(g) + e⁻","Mg(g) + e⁻ → Mg⁻(g)","Mg(g) → Mg²⁺(g) + 2e⁻","Mg⁺(g) → Mg²⁺(g) + e⁻","Mg(g) → Mg⁺(g) + e⁻"],4,
 "IE₁ removes one electron from a gaseous atom: Mg(g) → Mg⁺(g) + e⁻. Starting from the solid adds sublimation; gaining an electron is electron affinity; removing two at once is IE₁ + IE₂; Mg⁺ → Mg²⁺ is IE₂.",
 ["Gaseous atom, one electron removed.","Check the state and the charges.","Mg(g) → Mg⁺(g)."]],
["m7-a-108",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which equation represents the electron affinity of sulfur?",
 ["S(g) → S⁺(g) + e⁻","S(s) + e⁻ → S⁻(s)","S⁻(g) + e⁻ → S²⁻(g)","S(g) + e⁻ → S⁻(g)","S(g) + 2e⁻ → S²⁻(g)"],3,
 "Electron affinity: one electron added to a gaseous atom, X(g) + e⁻ → X⁻(g). S(g) → S⁺ is ionization; the solid is the wrong state; S⁻ + e⁻ is the SECOND electron affinity; adding two electrons at once combines EA₁ and EA₂.",
 ["Electron affinity adds an electron.","Gaseous atom, one electron.","S(g) + e⁻ → S⁻(g)."]],
["m7-a-109",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which equation represents the SECOND ionization energy of calcium?",
 ["Ca(g) → Ca²⁺(g) + 2e⁻","Ca⁺(g) → Ca²⁺(g) + e⁻","Ca(g) → Ca⁺(g) + e⁻","Ca²⁺(g) → Ca³⁺(g) + e⁻","Ca(s) → Ca²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻"],1,
 "IE₂ removes one electron from the gaseous 1+ ion: Ca⁺(g) → Ca²⁺(g) + e⁻. Removing two electrons from Ca is IE₁ + IE₂ combined; Ca → Ca⁺ is IE₁; Ca²⁺ → Ca³⁺ is IE₃; the aqueous process is not an ionization energy.",
 ["Each ionization energy removes exactly one electron.","IE₂ starts from the 1+ ion.","Ca⁺(g) → Ca²⁺(g) + e⁻."]],
["m7-a-110",7,14,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "What is the general trend in first ionization energy in the periodic table?",
 ["Decreases across a period and increases down a group","Increases across a period and increases down a group","Decreases across a period and decreases down a group","Increases across a period (left to right) and decreases down a group","There is no general trend."],3,
 "IE₁ is roughly the opposite of atomic radius: across a period Zeff rises and electrons are held more tightly (IE up); down a group the valence electron is farther away (IE down). Highest IE₁: upper right (He); lowest: lower left (Cs, Fr).",
 ["IE tracks how tightly the outer electron is held.","Small atoms hold their electrons tightly.","Opposite of the radius trend."]],
["m7-a-111",7,14,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which element has the HIGHEST first ionization energy?",
 ["Ne","He","F","Ar","H"],1,
 "He’s two 1s electrons are very close to the nucleus with no shielding by inner electrons, giving the highest IE₁ of all elements (2372 kJ/mol). Ne (2081) is next; F (1681), Ar (1521) and H (1312) are lower.",
 ["Highest IE is at the top right of the table.","Noble gases beat their neighbours.","The smallest noble gas."]],
["m7-a-112",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Rank K, Na, Li and Be in order of INCREASING first ionization energy.",
 ["Be < Li < Na < K","K < Na < Be < Li","Li < Na < K < Be","Na < K < Li < Be","K < Na < Li < Be"],4,
 "Down group 1 IE₁ decreases: K (419) < Na (496) < Li (520) kJ/mol. Be is to the right of Li in period 2, so it is higher still (899). The first choice is the reverse; the others mix the trends.",
 ["Use the group trend for K, Na, Li.","Then compare Li and Be across period 2.","Lower left = lowest IE."]],
["m7-a-113",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why is the first ionization energy of boron LOWER than that of beryllium, even though B has a higher nuclear charge?",
 ["B has a larger nuclear charge, which makes its electrons easier to remove.","B’s outer electron is a paired 2p electron that is repelled by its partner.","B’s outer electron is in a 2p orbital, which is higher in energy than Be’s 2s (it penetrates less and is shielded by the 2s pair).","B is larger than Be because it has more electrons.","Be has a half-filled 2s sublevel."],2,
 "Be = 1s² 2s²; B = 1s² 2s² 2p¹. B’s electron removed comes from 2p, a higher-energy sublevel (shielded by the 2s electrons), so it is easier to remove: IE₁(B) 801 < IE₁(Be) 899 kJ/mol. The single 2p electron of B is unpaired, so pairing cannot be the reason - pairing explains the O vs N dip, not B vs Be. Be’s 2s is full, not half-filled.",
 ["Compare the sublevel each electron is removed from.","Be loses a 2s electron; B loses a 2p electron.","2p is higher in energy than 2s."]],
["m7-a-114",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why is the first ionization energy of oxygen LOWER than that of nitrogen?",
 ["O has a smaller nuclear charge than N.","O’s outer electron is in a higher sublevel, 3s.","N’s 2p electrons are paired, which makes them more stable.","O’s 2p⁴ has one doubly occupied orbital; repulsion between the paired electrons makes one of them easier to remove.","O is larger than N."],3,
 "N = 2p³ (one electron in each 2p orbital, half-filled); O = 2p⁴ (one orbital holds a pair). The paired electrons repel each other, so removing one costs less: IE₁(O) 1314 < IE₁(N) 1402 kJ/mol. O has MORE protons, its electron is still 2p, N’s 2p electrons are unpaired, and O is actually slightly smaller.",
 ["Draw the 2p boxes for N and O.","Which atom has two electrons sharing an orbital?","Paired-electron repulsion lowers the IE."]],
["m7-a-115",7,14,4,"Concept","mcq",
 "In each pair, which element has the LARGER first ionization energy? (i) P or S (ii) Mg or Al (iii) Na or K",
 ["P, Mg, Na","S, Al, Na","S, Mg, K","P, Al, K","S, Al, K"],0,
 "(i) P > S: S removes a paired 3p electron (group 15/16 dip). (ii) Mg > Al: Al removes a higher-energy 3p electron (group 2/13 dip). (iii) Na > K: K’s valence electron is in n = 4, farther out. Choosing S and Al applies the simple ‘increases across’ trend and misses both exceptions.",
 ["Two of these pairs are the famous exceptions.","Group 2 beats group 13; group 15 beats group 16.","Down a group IE drops."]],
["m7-a-116",7,14,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Rank Na, Mg, Al and Si in order of INCREASING first ionization energy.",
 ["Na < Mg < Al < Si","Si < Al < Mg < Na","Na < Al < Mg < Si","Al < Na < Mg < Si","Na < Mg < Si < Al"],2,
 "IE₁ (kJ/mol): Na 496, Al 578, Mg 738, Si 786. The general trend predicts Na < Mg < Al < Si, but Al is below Mg because Al’s electron comes from the higher-energy 3p sublevel. Na is still lowest - Al’s 3p electron is held more tightly than Na’s 3s electron because Al has a much higher Zeff.",
 ["Start with the general trend, then look for an exception.","Group 13 is lower than group 2 in the same period.","Swap Mg and Al from the simple order."]],
["m7-a-117",7,14,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Rank C, N, O and F in order of INCREASING first ionization energy.",
 ["C < N < O < F","F < O < N < C","C < O < F < N","O < C < N < F","C < O < N < F"],4,
 "IE₁ (kJ/mol): C 1086, O 1314, N 1402, F 1681. O falls below N because its fourth 2p electron is paired. O is still above C (higher Zeff). The simple trend C < N < O < F misses the N/O exception.",
 ["Expect the overall increase across period 2.","Which pair is the group 15/16 exception?","Swap N and O."]],
["m7-a-118",7,14,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Which order shows Mg, Al, P, S and Cl in INCREASING first ionization energy?",
 ["Mg < Al < P < S < Cl","Al < Mg < S < P < Cl","Al < Mg < P < S < Cl","Mg < Al < S < P < Cl","Cl < S < P < Al < Mg"],1,
 "IE₁ (kJ/mol): Al 578 < Mg 738 < S 1000 < P 1012 < Cl 1251. Both period-3 exceptions apply: group 13 below group 2 (Al’s 3p electron is higher in energy) and group 16 below group 15 (S removes a paired 3p electron). Each wrong order misses one or both exceptions, or reverses the trend.",
 ["Apply the general increase, then fix two exceptions.","Al below Mg; S below P.","Al < Mg, then S < P, then Cl."]],
["m7-a-119",7,14,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "A period-3 element has these successive ionization energies (kJ/mol): IE₁ = 738, IE₂ = 1451, IE₃ = 7733, IE₄ = 10543. To which group does it belong?",
 ["Group 1","Group 13","Group 14","Group 2","Group 3"],3,
 "The huge jump is between IE₂ (1451) and IE₃ (7733), more than 5×. So the first two electrons are valence and the third comes from the core: 2 valence electrons, group 2 (it is Mg, forming Mg²⁺). Group 1 would jump after IE₁; group 13 after IE₃.",
 ["Find where the big jump is.","The jump marks the first core electron.","Two easy electrons, then the core."]],
["m7-a-120",7,14,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "A period-3 element has these successive ionization energies (kJ/mol): 578, 1817, 2745, 11577, 14842. To which group does it belong?",
 ["Group 2","Group 3","Group 15","Group 14","Group 13"],4,
 "The jump is from IE₃ (2745) to IE₄ (11577), about 4×. Three valence electrons, then the core: group 13 (it is aluminum, which forms Al³⁺). In period 3 there is no group 3 element - the transition metals start in period 4.",
 ["Locate the largest ratio between neighbours.","2745 → 11577 is the jump.","Three valence electrons in a main-group element."]],
["m7-a-121",7,14,3,"Exam","mcq",
 "An element has these successive ionization energies (kJ/mol): 786, 1577, 3232, 4356, 16091, 19784. To which group does it belong?",
 ["Group 2","Group 13","Group 14","Group 15","Group 16"],2,
 "IEs rise steadily for the first four (each a bit more than the last), then jump from 4356 to 16091 (about 3.7×). Four valence electrons → group 14 (this is silicon). The rises from IE₁ to IE₄ are normal; only the jump into the core matters.",
 ["Do not be fooled by the steady increase - look for the BIG jump.","4356 → 16091.","Four valence electrons."]],
["m7-a-122",7,14,3,"Exam","fill",
 "A period-3 element has IE₁ = 496 kJ/mol, IE₂ = 4562 kJ/mol and IE₃ = 6910 kJ/mol. Identify the element. (Type the element symbol.)",
 ["Na","sodium"],null,
 "The jump comes immediately after IE₁ (496 → 4562, more than 9×), so there is one valence electron: group 1. In period 3 that is sodium, [Ne] 3s¹. Its second electron would come from the 2p core.",
 ["Where is the big jump?","Jump right after IE₁ → one valence electron.","Group 1, period 3."]],
["m7-a-123",7,14,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Element Q is in period 3. Its successive ionization energies (kJ/mol) are 578, 1817, 2745 and 11577. What is the most likely formula of its oxide?",
 ["QO","Q₂O₃","Q₂O","QO₂","Q₃O₂"],1,
 "The jump after IE₃ shows 3 valence electrons, so Q forms Q³⁺. Oxide ion O²⁻. Balance charges: 2 × (+3) = 3 × (−2) → Q₂O₃ (Q is Al; Al₂O₃). QO would be a 2+ ion, Q₂O a 1+ ion, QO₂ a 4+ ion; Q₃O₂ swaps the subscripts.",
 ["Use the jump to find the ion’s charge.","Q forms Q³⁺; oxide is O²⁻.","Criss-cross the charges."]],
["m7-a-124",7,14,3,"Exam","num",
 "An element in period 3 has successive ionization energies (kJ/mol) of 1012, 1907, 2914, 4964, 6274 and 21267. How many valence electrons does it have? (Enter a whole number.)",
 ["5"],5,
 "Ratios between neighbours: 1907/1012 ≈ 1.9, 2914/1907 ≈ 1.5, 4964/2914 ≈ 1.7, 6274/4964 ≈ 1.3, 21267/6274 ≈ 3.4. The big jump is between IE₅ and IE₆, so five electrons are valence (group 15, phosphorus). The rise from IE₃ to IE₄ is moderate: it reflects going from 3p to 3s electrons, not into the core.",
 ["Compute the ratio of each IE to the one before it.","The core jump is the largest ratio.","6274 → 21267."]],
["m7-a-125",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "For calcium, which successive ionization energy is MUCH larger than the one before it?",
 ["IE₂","IE₁","IE₄","IE₃","IE₂₀"],3,
 "Ca = [Ar] 4s². IE₁ and IE₂ remove the two 4s valence electrons; IE₃ removes a 3p core electron from the argon-like Ca²⁺, so IE₃ (4912) is much larger than IE₂ (1145) kJ/mol. That is why calcium forms Ca²⁺.",
 ["How many valence electrons does Ca have?","After two are gone, Ca²⁺ has a noble-gas core.","The third electron is the first core electron."]],
["m7-a-126",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why is each successive ionization energy of an atom LARGER than the one before it?",
 ["The nucleus gains a proton after each ionization.","Electrons are always removed from the lowest n first.","Each electron is removed from an increasingly positive ion, so the remaining electrons are held more tightly.","Removing an electron increases the shielding of the others.","The ion gets larger after each ionization."],2,
 "After each electron is lost, the same nuclear charge holds fewer electrons, with less repulsion, and the electron must be pulled away from a more positive ion - so IE₁ < IE₂ < IE₃ … The nucleus does not change; electrons come from the highest n first; shielding decreases and the ion gets smaller.",
 ["What is the charge of the species each electron leaves?","Each step removes an electron from a more positive ion.","Same protons, fewer electrons, tighter hold."]],
["m7-a-127",7,14,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which atom has the LARGEST second ionization energy, IE₂?",
 ["Mg","Ca","Al","K","Na"],4,
 "IE₂ is large when the second electron comes from a noble-gas core. Na⁺ = [Ne] and K⁺ = [Ar], so both have huge IE₂; Na’s core electron is closer to the nucleus (n = 2 vs n = 3), so Na’s IE₂ (4562 kJ/mol) beats K’s (3052). Mg, Ca and Al still remove a valence electron at IE₂ (1451, 1145, 1817). Choosing Mg or Al assumes IE₂ follows the same across-the-period trend as IE₁.",
 ["For which atoms does the second electron come from the core?","Group 1 atoms: Na⁺ and K⁺ are noble-gas ions.","Of Na⁺ and K⁺, which core electron is closer to the nucleus?"]],
["m7-a-128",7,14,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["Within a period, the atom with the smallest radius usually has one of the largest first ionization energies.","The first ionization energy of nitrogen is greater than that of oxygen.","IE₂ of sodium is much larger than its IE₁ because the second electron comes from the n = 2 core.","Since potassium is larger than sodium, potassium has the larger first ionization energy.","Noble gases have the highest first ionization energies in their periods."],3,
 "Larger atoms have SMALLER ionization energies: K’s 4s electron is farther from the nucleus than Na’s 3s, so IE₁(K) = 419 < IE₁(Na) = 496 kJ/mol. That is the classic confusion of a large radius with a large IE. Every other statement is true.",
 ["IE and radius trends run in opposite directions.","Is a farther-out electron easier or harder to remove?","Bigger atom → lower IE₁."]],
["m7-a-129",7,14,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Electron affinity is the energy change when:",
 ["one mole of gaseous atoms loses one mole of electrons","one mole of electrons is added to one mole of gaseous atoms (or ions)","an atom in a bond attracts the shared electrons toward itself","one mole of an ionic solid is separated into gaseous ions","an atom absorbs a photon"],1,
 "EA: X(g) + e⁻ → X⁻(g), per mole. Losing electrons is ionization energy; attracting shared bonding electrons is electronegativity (Chapter 9); separating an ionic solid is lattice energy.",
 ["The opposite process to ionization.","An electron is ADDED.","X(g) + e⁻ → X⁻(g)."]],
["m7-a-130",7,14,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which element has the MOST NEGATIVE electron affinity (releases the most energy when it gains an electron)?",
 ["F","O","Br","Cl","I"],3,
 "Chlorine: about −349 kJ/mol, slightly more negative than fluorine (about −328). F is so small that the incoming electron is crowded into a compact 2p shell with strong electron-electron repulsion, which offsets its high Zeff. Halogens beat group 16 (O about −141), and down the group from Cl the EA becomes less negative (Br −325, I −295).",
 ["The halogens have the most negative EAs - but which one?","Fluorine’s small 2p shell causes extra repulsion.","The second halogen is the record holder."]],
["m7-a-131",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why is the electron affinity of nitrogen much less negative (close to zero) than those of carbon and oxygen?",
 ["N has a smaller nuclear charge than C and O.","N’s extra electron would have to go into a 3s orbital.","N is a gas at room temperature.","N has the largest atomic radius in period 2.","The added electron must pair up in one of N’s half-filled 2p orbitals, which raises electron-electron repulsion."],4,
 "N = 2p³, one electron in each 2p orbital. An added electron must pair up, and the extra repulsion cancels most of the attraction, so EA(N) is near zero (slightly positive). C (2p²) can add an electron to an empty 2p orbital (−122 kJ/mol), and O’s high Zeff wins out (−141). N has more protons than C; the electron would go into 2p, not 3s; physical state is irrelevant; Li is the largest period-2 atom.",
 ["Draw the 2p boxes of N.","Every 2p orbital already has one electron.","Pairing costs repulsion energy."]],
["m7-a-132",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Why is the SECOND electron affinity of oxygen, O⁻(g) + e⁻ → O²⁻(g), positive?",
 ["O²⁻ has an unfilled 2p sublevel.","Oxygen’s nucleus attracts the second electron more strongly than the first.","The incoming electron is repelled by the negative charge of O⁻, so energy must be absorbed to add it.","All electron affinities are positive.","It is not positive; O²⁻ forms spontaneously from O⁻ in the gas phase."],2,
 "Adding an electron to an already negative ion means pushing it against electron-electron repulsion, so energy is absorbed (EA₂ > 0). O²⁻ still forms in ionic solids like MgO because the very large lattice energy pays for it. O²⁻ has a FILLED 2p sublevel; most first EAs are negative.",
 ["What is the charge of the species receiving the second electron?","A negative ion repels another electron.","Energy must be put in."]],
["m7-a-133",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which group of elements has POSITIVE electron affinities (energy must be absorbed to add an electron)?",
 ["The halogens (group 17)","The noble gases (group 18)","The chalcogens (group 16)","The alkali metals (group 1)","The carbon group (group 14)"],1,
 "A noble gas has a filled outer s and p shell, so an added electron must start a new, much higher-energy shell (n + 1), far from the nucleus and well shielded: energy is absorbed. Groups 1, 14, 16 and 17 all have negative (exothermic) first EAs; the halogens are the most negative. (Be and Mg in group 2 also have positive EAs, because their filled ns² forces the added electron into the higher np sublevel; the heavier group 2 atoms Ca, Sr and Ba have EAs only slightly below zero, so group 2 as a whole is not the answer.)",
 ["Where would the added electron have to go?","Which atoms have a completely filled outer s and p?","A new shell must be started."]],
["m7-a-134",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which process RELEASES the most energy?",
 ["Na(g) → Na⁺(g) + e⁻","O⁻(g) + e⁻ → O²⁻(g)","Ne(g) + e⁻ → Ne⁻(g)","Cl(g) + e⁻ → Cl⁻(g)","N(g) + e⁻ → N⁻(g)"],3,
 "Cl(g) + e⁻ → Cl⁻(g) has ΔE ≈ −349 kJ/mol (the most negative EA of any element). Ionizing Na absorbs energy (+496); O⁻ → O²⁻ absorbs energy (second EA); adding an electron to Ne absorbs energy (new shell); N’s EA is about zero.",
 ["Ionization always absorbs energy - rule that out.","Which electron additions are exothermic?","Halogens have the most negative EAs."]],
["m7-a-135",7,14,1,"Concept","mcq",
 "Metallic character in the periodic table increases:",
 ["up a group and to the right","up a group and to the left","down a group and to the left","down a group and to the right","only across periods, not within groups"],2,
 "Metallic behaviour (losing electrons easily, low IE) increases down a group and decreases across a period, so the most metallic elements are at the lower left (Cs, Fr) and the most nonmetallic at the upper right (F).",
 ["Metals lose electrons easily - where is IE lowest?","IE is lowest at the lower left.","Down and left."]],
["m7-a-136",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Rank Si, Na, Mg and Cs in order of INCREASING metallic character.",
 ["Si < Na < Mg < Cs","Cs < Na < Mg < Si","Mg < Si < Na < Cs","Na < Mg < Si < Cs","Si < Mg < Na < Cs"],4,
 "Across period 3, metallic character decreases Na > Mg > Si (Si is a metalloid). Cs is below Na in group 1, so it is the most metallic. Increasing order: Si < Mg < Na < Cs. Putting Na below Mg reverses the period trend.",
 ["Metallic character follows the opposite of IE.","Period 3: Na more metallic than Mg, Mg more than Si.","Cs is the most metallic of the four."]],
["m7-a-137",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which oxide dissolves in water to give an ACIDIC solution?",
 ["Na₂O","CaO","SO₃","MgO","K₂O"],2,
 "Nonmetal oxides are acidic: SO₃(g) + H₂O(l) → H₂SO₄(aq). The metal oxides are basic: Na₂O + H₂O → 2NaOH, CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂, K₂O + H₂O → 2KOH, and MgO gives Mg(OH)₂.",
 ["Is each oxide a metal oxide or a nonmetal oxide?","Nonmetal oxides form acids with water.","S is the only nonmetal here."]],
["m7-a-138",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which oxide dissolves in water to give a BASIC solution?",
 ["CO₂","SO₂","N₂O₅","BaO","P₄O₁₀"],3,
 "BaO is a metal (group 2) oxide: BaO(s) + H₂O(l) → Ba(OH)₂(aq), a strong base. The rest are nonmetal oxides that give acids: H₂CO₃, H₂SO₃, HNO₃ and H₃PO₄.",
 ["Basic oxides are metal oxides.","Which formula contains a metal?","Group 2 oxide + water → hydroxide."]],
["m7-a-139",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "What forms when sulfur trioxide reacts with water, SO₃(g) + H₂O(l) → ?",
 ["H₂SO₄(aq), so SO₃ is an acidic oxide","H₂SO₃(aq), so SO₃ is an acidic oxide","S(s) + H₂O₂(aq)","SO₃ is a basic oxide and forms S(OH)₂","No reaction occurs, because SO₃ is amphoteric"],0,
 "SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄ (sulfuric acid): the oxidation state of S (+6) and all the atoms are conserved. H₂SO₃ (sulfurous acid) comes from SO₂, not SO₃. Nonmetal oxides are acidic, not basic or amphoteric.",
 ["Add the atoms of SO₃ and H₂O.","S + 4 O + 2 H.","That is sulfuric acid, H₂SO₄."]],
["m7-a-140",7,14,3,"Calculation","mcq",
 "Tetraphosphorus decaoxide reacts with water to form phosphoric acid: P₄O₁₀(s) + __ H₂O(l) → __ H₃PO₄(aq). How many moles of water react and how many moles of H₃PO₄ form per mole of P₄O₁₀?",
 ["3 mol H₂O, 2 mol H₃PO₄","6 mol H₂O, 4 mol H₃PO₄","4 mol H₂O, 4 mol H₃PO₄","10 mol H₂O, 4 mol H₃PO₄","6 mol H₂O, 2 mol H₃PO₄"],1,
 "Balance P first: 4 P → 4 H₃PO₄. That needs 12 H → 6 H₂O. Check O: 10 + 6 = 16 = 4 × 4. So P₄O₁₀ + 6H₂O → 4H₃PO₄, an acidic-oxide reaction. Every other choice fails the P, H or O count.",
 ["Balance phosphorus first.","4 H₃PO₄ contain 12 H.","12 H come from 6 H₂O; check the oxygens."]],
["m7-a-141",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which oxide is AMPHOTERIC (reacts with both acids and bases)?",
 ["Na₂O","SO₂","Al₂O₃","CO₂","MgO"],2,
 "Al₂O₃ is the classic amphoteric oxide: it dissolves in acid (forming Al³⁺) and in strong base (forming Al(OH)₄⁻). Na₂O and MgO are basic; SO₂ and CO₂ are acidic.",
 ["Amphoteric oxides sit near the metal/nonmetal boundary.","Think of the group 13 metal in period 3.","Aluminum oxide."]],
["m7-a-142",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which pair of equations shows that aluminum oxide is amphoteric?",
 ["Al₂O₃(s) + 6HCl(aq) → 2AlCl₃(aq) + 3H₂O(l) and Al₂O₃(s) + 2NaOH(aq) + 3H₂O(l) → 2NaAl(OH)₄(aq)","Al₂O₃(s) + 3H₂O(l) → 2Al(OH)₃(s) only","Al₂O₃(s) + 6HCl(aq) → 2AlCl₃(aq) + 3H₂O(l) only","Al₂O₃(s) + 2NaOH(aq) + 3H₂O(l) → 2NaAl(OH)₄(aq) only","2Al₂O₃(s) → 4Al(s) + 3O₂(g)"],0,
 "Amphoteric means it reacts both as a base (with an acid, HCl) and as an acid (with a base, NaOH). You need BOTH reactions; either one alone only shows basic or acidic behaviour. Decomposition to the elements says nothing about acid-base behaviour.",
 ["Amphoteric = both acid and base behaviour.","It must react with an acid AND with a base.","Pick the choice showing both."]],
["m7-a-143",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which list arranges the period-3 oxides from MOST BASIC to MOST ACIDIC?",
 ["Na₂O, MgO, Al₂O₃, SiO₂, P₄O₁₀, SO₃, Cl₂O₇","Cl₂O₇, SO₃, P₄O₁₀, SiO₂, Al₂O₃, MgO, Na₂O","Al₂O₃, MgO, Na₂O, SiO₂, P₄O₁₀, SO₃, Cl₂O₇","MgO, Na₂O, Al₂O₃, SiO₂, SO₃, P₄O₁₀, Cl₂O₇","Na₂O, Al₂O₃, MgO, SiO₂, P₄O₁₀, Cl₂O₇, SO₃"],0,
 "Across a period, metallic character falls, so oxides go from strongly basic (Na₂O), through basic (MgO) and amphoteric (Al₂O₃), to increasingly acidic (SiO₂, P₄O₁₀, SO₃, and Cl₂O₇, which gives the strong acid HClO₄). The second choice is the same list reversed (most acidic first).",
 ["Oxide acidity follows nonmetallic character.","Left to right across period 3: basic → amphoteric → acidic.","Follow the order of the elements Na → Cl."]],
["m7-a-144",7,14,4,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which statement about the acid-base behaviour of oxides is FALSE?",
 ["Oxides become more acidic from left to right across a period.","Oxides of a group become more basic going down the group.","Nonmetal oxides react with water to form acids.","ZnO and Al₂O₃ react with both acids and bases.","CO₂ dissolves in water to give a basic solution."],4,
 "CO₂ is a nonmetal oxide: CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃, carbonic acid, so its solution is ACIDIC (that is why soda water is slightly acidic). The other statements are true: acidity rises across a period, basicity rises down a group (metallic character), and ZnO and Al₂O₃ are amphoteric.",
 ["Check each statement against the metal/nonmetal rule.","Carbon is a nonmetal.","CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃."]],
["m7-a-145",7,14,2,"Concept","mcq",
 "Calcium oxide (lime) is added to water. Which statement is correct?",
 ["CaO(s) + H₂O(l) → Ca(OH)₂(aq); CaO is a basic oxide.","CaO(s) + H₂O(l) → CaH₂(s) + O₂(g); CaO is a basic oxide.","CaO(s) + H₂O(l) → H₂CaO₂(aq), an acid; CaO is an acidic oxide.","CaO is amphoteric and does not react with water.","CaO(s) + H₂O(l) → Ca(s) + H₂O₂(aq); CaO is neutral."],0,
 "A group 2 metal oxide reacts with water to give the metal hydroxide: CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂, a base (slaked lime). Metal oxides are basic, not acidic; only a few metal oxides, such as Al₂O₃ and ZnO, are amphoteric.",
 ["Is Ca a metal or a nonmetal?","Metal oxide + water → metal hydroxide.","Ca(OH)₂."]],
["m7-a-146",7,14,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Element X has the ground-state configuration [Ne] 3s². Which statement about X is TRUE?",
 ["X forms the ion X²⁺, which is isoelectronic with Ne, and its oxide XO is basic.","X forms the ion X²⁻, and its oxide is acidic.","X has 2 unpaired electrons and is paramagnetic.","X’s oxide X₂O₃ is amphoteric.","X has a smaller first ionization energy than Al because its electrons are paired."],0,
 "X is magnesium. It loses its two 3s electrons to form Mg²⁺ ([Ne], isoelectronic with neon); MgO is a basic metal oxide. 3s² is paired (diamagnetic); X₂O₃ is aluminum’s oxide; and Mg’s IE₁ (738) is LARGER than Al’s (578), because Al’s electron comes from the higher-energy 3p sublevel - pairing is the reason for the N/O dip, not the Mg/Al one.",
 ["Identify X from its configuration.","[Ne] 3s² is Mg, a group 2 metal.","Metals form cations; metal oxides are basic."]],
["m7-a-147",7,14,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["Zn²⁺ is diamagnetic.","Fe³⁺ has 5 unpaired electrons.","Cu⁺ and Zn²⁺ are isoelectronic.","A ground-state Cr atom has 4 unpaired electrons.","Ti⁴⁺ is isoelectronic with Ar."],3,
 "Cr is [Ar] 4s¹ 3d⁵, with 6 unpaired electrons; 4 would come from the aufbau form [Ar] 4s² 3d⁴. The rest are true: Zn²⁺ = [Ar] 3d¹⁰ (all paired); Fe³⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁵ (5 unpaired); Cu⁺ and Zn²⁺ both have 28 electrons, [Ar] 3d¹⁰; Ti⁴⁺ = [Ar].",
 ["Check each configuration carefully - one uses a famous exception.","Remove 4s first for ions; remember Cr is 4s¹ 3d⁵.","Count Cr’s unpaired electrons from its real configuration."]],
["m7-a-148",7,13,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "A period-4 element is in the p-block and its ground-state atom has THREE unpaired electrons. Which element is it?",
 ["V","As","Co","P","Ga"],1,
 "A p-block atom with three unpaired electrons has np³ (Hund: one per p orbital). In period 4 that is 4p³: arsenic, [Ar] 4s² 3d¹⁰ 4p³. V (3d³) and Co (3d⁷) also have three unpaired electrons but are d-block; P is 3p³ in period 3; Ga (4p¹) has one.",
 ["Three unpaired p electrons means a half-filled p set.","np³ is group 15.","Group 15, period 4."]],
["m7-a-149",7,14,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which statement comparing sodium and chlorine atoms is FALSE?",
 ["The valence electrons of Cl feel a larger effective nuclear charge than that of Na.","Cl has a smaller atomic radius than Na.","Cl has a larger first ionization energy than Na.","Cl has a more negative electron affinity than Na.","Cl has more core electrons than Na, so its valence electrons are more shielded."],4,
 "Both are in period 3 and both have the same 10-electron [Ne] core, so core shielding is the same; Cl simply has six more protons. That is WHY Cl has the higher Zeff (≈ +7 vs ≈ +1), the smaller radius, the larger IE₁ (1251 vs 496 kJ/mol) and the more negative EA (−349 vs −53 kJ/mol). The false statement is the one about extra core electrons.",
 ["Count the core electrons of each.","Both have the [Ne] core.","Same core, more protons → stronger pull, not more shielding."]],
["m7-a-150",7,13,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "The configuration 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3d¹ represents:",
 ["the ground state of scandium","an impossible configuration, because 3d cannot be occupied before 4s","an excited state of sodium","the ground state of sodium","an excited state of aluminum"],2,
 "Electrons: 2 + 2 + 6 + 1 = 11, so this is sodium. Ground-state Na is [Ne] 3s¹; here the valence electron has been promoted to 3d, so it is an excited state. Any orbital can hold a promoted electron, so it is not impossible. Scandium has 21 electrons and Al has 13.",
 ["Count the electrons to identify the element.","11 electrons = Na. What is Na’s ground state?","The 3s electron has been promoted to 3d."]],
["m7-a-151",7,13,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Which set of quantum numbers (n, l, mₗ, mₛ) could describe one of the 2p electrons of a ground-state oxygen atom?",
 ["2, 1, 1, +½","2, 2, 1, +½","2, 0, 0, +½","1, 1, 0, −½","2, 1, 0, 0"],0,
 "A 2p electron has n = 2, l = 1, mₗ = −1, 0 or +1, mₛ = ±½: (2, 1, 1, +½) works. (2, 2, …) is impossible (l must be less than n); (2, 0, 0) is a 2s electron, not 2p; (1, 1, …) is impossible; mₛ = 0 is never allowed.",
 ["What are n and l for a 2p electron?","n = 2, l = 1; then check mₗ and mₛ.","mₗ must be −1, 0 or +1 and mₛ must be ±½."]],
["m7-a-152",7,14,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Atoms X and Y are in the same period. X has the configuration [Ne] 3s² 3p³ and Y has [Ne] 3s² 3p⁴. Which statement is TRUE?",
 ["Y has the larger first ionization energy because it is farther right in the period.","X has the larger first ionization energy because Y loses an electron from a doubly occupied 3p orbital.","X and Y have the same number of unpaired electrons.","Y has a larger atomic radius than X.","X has the more negative electron affinity because its 3p sublevel is half-filled."],1,
 "X = P (3p³, 3 unpaired), Y = S (3p⁴, 2 unpaired). The group 15/16 exception: S’s electron is removed from a paired 3p orbital, so IE₁(S) 1000 < IE₁(P) 1012 kJ/mol. S is slightly SMALLER than P (higher Zeff), and P’s half-filled 3p makes its EA LESS negative (−72) than S’s (−200), because the added electron would have to pair.",
 ["Identify X and Y and draw their 3p boxes.","X = P (3p³), Y = S (3p⁴).","Which atom has a paired electron that is easier to remove?"]],
["m7-a-153",7,14,4,"Exam","mcq",
 "Which ion is NOT isoelectronic with krypton?",
 ["Rb⁺","Sr²⁺","Br⁻","Se²⁻","Cs⁺"],4,
 "Kr has 36 electrons. Rb⁺ (37 − 1), Sr²⁺ (38 − 2), Br⁻ (35 + 1) and Se²⁻ (34 + 2) all have 36. Cs⁺ has 55 − 1 = 54 electrons - it is isoelectronic with xenon. Cs is ‘like Rb’ chemically, which is the trap.",
 ["Electrons = Z − charge.","Krypton has 36 electrons.","Which ion has 54?"]],
["m7-a-154",7,14,3,"Concept","mcq",
 "Rank Al³⁺, Mg²⁺, Na⁺ and F⁻ in order of INCREASING ionic radius.",
 ["F⁻ < Na⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Al³⁺","Na⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Al³⁺ < F⁻","Al³⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Na⁺ < F⁻","Al³⁺ < Na⁺ < Mg²⁺ < F⁻","F⁻ < Al³⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Na⁺"],2,
 "All four have 10 electrons. The more protons, the smaller: Al (13) < Mg (12) < Na (11) < F (9). So Al³⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Na⁺ < F⁻. Ranking by ‘bigger Z → bigger ion’ gives the reverse.",
 ["They are isoelectronic - check.","Compare nuclear charges.","Highest Z = smallest."]],
["m7-a-155",7,13,4,"Trap","mcq",
 "Which statement about the 4s and 3d orbitals is TRUE?",
 ["In K and Ca the 3d orbitals are lower in energy than 4s, which is why 3d fills first.","Because 4s fills before 3d, transition metals also lose 3d electrons before 4s when they form cations.","4s fills before 3d in K and Ca, but once 3d is occupied the 4s electrons are the outermost ones and are lost first, so Fe²⁺ is [Ar] 3d⁶.","The 4s electrons are lost first only for Fe and Co; other transition metals lose 3d first.","Ions keep their 4s electrons because 4s is lower in energy than 3d in every species."],2,
 "In K and Ca, 4s is lower than 3d (penetration), so it fills first. But 4s has the larger n and is the outermost orbital, and in the transition-metal ions the 3d level is the lower one - so ionization always takes the 4s electrons first, for EVERY transition metal: Fe²⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁶, Zn²⁺ = [Ar] 3d¹⁰. ‘Last in, first out’ is the trap.",
 ["Filling order and removal order are not mirror images.","Which electrons are farthest from the nucleus in Fe?","ns electrons always leave first."]],
["m7-a-156",7,14,3,"Calculation","num",
 "How many unpaired electrons does the Co³⁺ ion have in its ground state (free ion, Hund’s rule)? (Enter a whole number.)",
 ["4"],4,
 "Co = [Ar] 4s² 3d⁷ → Co³⁺ loses both 4s electrons and one 3d electron: [Ar] 3d⁶. Six d electrons in five orbitals: five singles, then one pairs → 4 unpaired.",
 ["Write Co, then remove three electrons (4s first).","Co³⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁶.","One pair plus four singles."]],
]);
